one, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b09fdbb-66b9-5dfe-826b-d54a738d1894', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b09fdbb-66b9-5dfe-826b-d54a738d1894'}, {'abstract': '“Jazz for Young People: The Resilient Cities Tour” will link live performances to American history, and visit 60 schools in five cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/01/arts/music/jazz-at-lincoln-center-and-rockefeller-foundation-pair-up-for-school-tour.html', 'snippet': '“Jazz for Young People: The Resilient Cities Tour” will link live performances to American history, and visit 60 schools in five cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center and the Rockefeller Foundation have announced a new education outreach program for students in disadvantaged schools. The program, “Jazz for Young People: The Resilient Cities Tour,” will connect live jazz performances to an American history curriculum, reaching 60 schools in five cities: Chicago; New Orleans; St. Louis; San Juan, P.R.; and Sydney, Australia.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center and Rockefeller Foundation Pair Up for School Tour', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center Creates New Outreach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockefeller Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodin, Judith S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-01T02:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a997c95-aa2e-526a-b1bd-96eac1a64bc9', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a997c95-aa2e-526a-b1bd-96eac1a64bc9'}, {'abstract': 'Naz is realizing he has to figure out the motives of not only other inmates, but also those of his lawyer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/07/31/arts/television/the-night-of-season-1-episode-4-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Naz is realizing he has to figure out the motives of not only other inmates, but also those of his lawyer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Nazir Khan, Riz Ahmed has excelled at playing a passive character, which is one of the more difficult assignments for an actor — or at least one of the more thankless, given the absence of emotional pyrotechnics. Fear and desire have motivated Naz, as Nazir is known, at every turn, leaving him paralyzed to the point where he’s drifted through his time with Andrea and his time in the justice system in the same trance-like state of shock. Though the time between when he slept with Andrea and when we woke up downstairs in the kitchen has not been accounted for, his innocence seems assured because he does not seem capable of taking the aggressive, decisive action necessary to commit such a horrific crime of passion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/07/31/watching/31nightof/31nightof-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/07/31/watching/31nightof/31nightof-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarg