okends-promo/07bookends-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/books/review/07bookends-promo/07bookends-promo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1244, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Do So Many Contemporary American Authors Send Their Characters Abroad?', 'kicker': 'Bookends', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Do So Many Contemporary American Authors Send Their Characters Abroad?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-02T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2abfa70d-1e6c-52c5-885f-f1ae540ac9f9', 'word_count': 1598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2abfa70d-1e6c-52c5-885f-f1ae540ac9f9'}, {'abstract': 'Critics say nothing has changed, but the truth is that American banks really are taking on less risk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/magazine/has-wall-street-been-tamed.html', 'snippet': 'Critics say nothing has changed, but the truth is that American banks really are taking on less risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the financial crisis, bankers were often regarded as greedy and self-\xadinterested. The mortgage melt down \xadratcheted that reputation into something much scarier: an existential threat to the global economy. Since 2008, the banks have seemed to many people like an active volcano does to the villagers below. Americans don’t really understand banks, or know what causes them to blow up, but they’ve seen enough to be afraid.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 528, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 528}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1253, 'width': 1425, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07onmoney1/07mag-07onmoney-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Has Wall Street Been Tamed?', 'kicker': 'On Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Has Wall Street Been Tamed?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Popper, Nathaniel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-02T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathaniel Popper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathaniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fea5199e-1b1d-50c8-8164-752134406437', 'word_count': 1448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fea5199e-1b1d-50c8-8164-752134406437'}, {'abstract': 'Self-congratulatory discussions inevitably follow moments of discord in the United States. So why aren’t they getting us anywhere?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/magazine/why-calls-for-a-national-conversation-are-futile.html', 'snippet': 'Self-congratulatory discussions inevitably follow moments of discord in the United States. So why aren’t they getting us anywhere?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bad news never seems to cease: death, discrimination, discrimination that culminates in death. Within days in early July, police officers shot and killed Alton Sterling in Baton Rouge, La., then Philando Castile near St. Paul, Minn., both of them black men. On July 7, a sniper murdered five cops during an otherwise peaceful protest in Dallas. Ten days later a different gunman killed three officers in Baton Rouge. This all took place not long after the June massacre at the Pulse nightclub in Orlando, Fla., and just before the campaign conventions of the country’s two political parties, during an election year that feels historically nasty. When things get this fractious and this tragic, we inevitably hear calls for a “national conversation.” But during Barack Obama’s presidency, news organizations, politicians and academics have scarcely had to do any calling. Whatever it is we need to talk about is simply added to the mounting agenda.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 566}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 967, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07firstwords/07firstwords-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1435, 'width': 1520, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Calls for a ‘National Conversation’ Are Futile', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talking Cure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Wesley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ghostbusters (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-02T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wesley Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wesley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2afb666-ad02-5053-9181-f638b268adb4', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2afb666-ad02-5053-9181-f638b268adb4'}, {'abstract': 'Five years after the tsunami that killed tens of thousands in Japan, a husband still searches the sea for his wife, joined by a father hoping to find his daughter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/magazine/the-lost-ones.html', 'snippet': 'Five years after the tsunami that killed tens of thousands in Japan, a husband still searches the sea for his wife, joined by a father hoping to find his daughter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yuko Takamatsu was somewhere in the sea off the coast of Japan. Two and a half years had passed since the tsunami, and no one had found her; but no one was really looking, either, except her husband, Yasuo Takamatsu, who loved her very much. Takamatsu first searched on land, at the bank where she vanished, and along the beaches of Onagawa, and in the forests in the mountains. After two and half years, in September 2013, when he still hadn’t found her, he turned to the sea.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/magazine/07japan1/07japan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘I Have No Choice but to Keep Looking’', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lost Ones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'su