ead’s new novel explores with brute realism and fablelike allegory a network of black and white activists who helped slaves escape to freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his dynamic new novel, Colson Whitehead takes the Underground Railroad — the loosely interlocking network of black and white activists who helped slaves escape to freedom in the decades before the Civil War — and turns it from a metaphor into an actual train that ferries fugitives northward.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 964, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 964}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 638, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1692, 'width': 1053, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03BOOKWHITEHEAD-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/02/books/03BOOKWHITEHEAD/03