g (2015)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roof, Dylann Storm', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-02T12:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike McPhate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McPhate', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1f46335-e805-5537-aec8-f6c688452a43', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1f46335-e805-5537-aec8-f6c688452a43'}, {'abstract': 'These days, one imagines happy tomorrows only after death, not before.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/02/opinion/paradise-the-new-muslim-utopia.html', 'snippet': 'These days, one imagines happy tomorrows only after death, not before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORAN, Algeria — Future writing project: a topography of paradise in the medieval Muslim imagination. But not only medieval, for among Muslims today paradise is also at the center of political discourse, sermons and the contemporary imagination. Paradise as a goal for the individual or the group has gradually replaced the dreams of development, stability and wealth promised by postwar decolonization in the so-called Arab world. These days, one imagines happy tomorrows only after death, not before.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 773}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1591, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paradise, the New Muslim Utopia', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-02T12:40:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kamel Daoud', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kamel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daoud', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d093d88-17fe-51d8-b3db-f60a6dcf4db9', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d093d88-17fe-51d8-b3db-f60a6dcf4db9'}, {'abstract': 'Désormais on ne parle des lendemains qui chantent que pour après la mort, pas avant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/02/opinion/le-paradis-musulman-nouvelle-utopie-politique.html', 'snippet': 'Désormais on ne parle des lendemains qui chantent que pour après la mort, pas avant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORAN, Algérie — A écrire un jour : une topographie du paradis dans l’imaginaire médiéval musulman. Pas seulement l’imaginaire médiéval d’ailleurs, puisque pour beaucoup de musulmans le paradis est aussi au centre du discours politique, du prêche et de l’imaginaire actuels. Le paradis comme but pour l’individu ou le groupe a remplacé peu à peu les rêves de développement, de stabilité et de richesse qu’avaient promis les décolonisations d’après-guerre dans le monde arabe. Désormais on ne parle des lendemains qui chantent que pour après la mort, pas avant.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 773}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1591, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/opinion/03EDDAOUD/03EDDAOUD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'cro