studio, which guarded the secret, to rise to renown in Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/arts/design/in-boston-a-major-della-robbia-show.html', 'snippet': 'Luca della Robbia invented a glazing technique for terra cotta that led to his family studio, which guarded the secret, to rise to renown in Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When  it was revealed this year that the British sculptor Anish Kapoor had secured the exclusive use in the art world of a material called Vantablack, a substance blacker than the inkiest black ever dreamed of by Manet or Ad Reinhardt, it led to some grumbling among colleagues.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 932}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1319, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Boston, a Major Della Robbia Show', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Terra Cotta  That Shines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Fine Arts (Boston)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'della Robbia, Luca', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-03T15:23:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e41d8a5-747b-53b0-a486-a900825a781e', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e41d8a5-747b-53b0-a486-a900825a781e'}, {'abstract': 'In “A Knight of the British Ballet,” at the Joyce Theater, the Sarasota Ballet will celebrate Ashton’s choreography from the 1930s to the 1970s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/arts/dance/fresh-from-florida-frederick-ashtons-british-ballet.html', 'snippet': 'In “A Knight of the British Ballet,” at the Joyce Theater, the Sarasota Ballet will celebrate Ashton’s choreography from the 1930s to the 1970s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not uncommon for Americans to retire to Florida, but who knew that the British choreographer Frederick Ashton (1904-88) would end up there, too? The Sarasota Ballet has become a significant repository for his work, which will be showcased in style when the company appears at the Joyce Theater. Beginning on Monday, Aug. 8, “A Knight of the British Ballet” will survey Ashton’s choreography, including “Facade” (1931), a tribute to popular songs and dances of the 1920s, and “Valses nobles et sentimentales” (1947). The program also features “Tweedledum and Tweedledee” (1977), a charming look at those characters; “The Walk to the Paradise Garden,” a homage to “Romeo and Juliet,” set to music by Frederick Delius (1972); and an excerpt from “Jazz Calendar,” called “Friday’s Child,” originally created for Antoinette Sibley and Rudolf Nureyev in 1968. There’s never enough Ashton shown in New York, but mercifully, Florida has come to the rescue. (Through Saturday, Aug. 13; 212-242-0800, joyce.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE1/07GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1160, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fresh From Florida, Frederick Ashton’s British Ballet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; Florida’s Home for British Ballet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joyce Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sarasota Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashton, Frederick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-03T15:26:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98e9c43a-75e4-56a5-b607-d88655f5b32c', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98e9c43a-75e4-56a5-b607-d88655f5b32c'}, {'abstract': 'Anthology Film Archives will show his “Nanook of the North” and “Man of Aran,” early documentaries that relied on imagination as much as on fact.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/movies/robert-j-flaherty-who-blurred-the-line-between-nonfiction-and-fiction-in-film.html', 'snippet': 'Anthology Film Archives will show his “Nanook of the North” and “Man of Aran,” early documentaries that relied on imagination as much as on fact.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert J. Flaherty is often called the father of the documentary: The term is said to have originated in a review of his “Moana” (1926). But veracity was not always his primary goal, as evidenced by his silent classic “Nanook of the North” (1922) and the talkie “Man of Aran” (1934), both heading to Anthology Film Archives.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/arts/07GUIDE9/07GUIDE9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1431, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert J. Flaherty, Who Blurred the Line Between Nonfiction and Fiction in Film', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film; True to the Spirit If Not the Letter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flaherty, Robert J (1884-1951)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANTHOLOGY FILM ARCHIVES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Man of Aran (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Nanook of the North (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-03T15:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c6165b-8d8a-5eb4-95ed-fe105eb52435', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c6165b-8d8a-5eb4-95ed-fe105eb52435'}, {'abstract': 'The spinning gold-plated copy of Carl Sagan’s “A Glorious Dawn” reached 94,000 feet above earth on a high-altitude balloon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/04/arts/music/third-man-records-space.html', 'snippet': 'The spinning gold-plated copy of Carl Sagan’s “A Glorious Dawn” reached 94,000 feet above earth on a high-altitude balloon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Third Man Records, the label in Nashville that was founded by Jack White, celebrated its seventh anniversary last month by launching a record into space. A gold-plated vinyl copy of Carl Sagan’s “A Glorious Dawn” spun on a turntable attached to a high-altitude balloon, reaching 94,000 feet above the earth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/arts/04THIRDMAN/04THIRDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/04/arts/04THIRDMAN/04THIRDMAN-articleLa