ntial)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Keauhou (Hawaii)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indianapolis (Ind)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austerlitz (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-03T21:31:28+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7d86ef4d-f204-59c0-aea9-5d394c5a3dd6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7d86ef4d-f204-59c0-aea9-5d394c5a3dd6'}, {'abstract': 'Mary L Chan of Bartleby Objects has no interest in making a plain black purse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/03/t-magazine/fashion/bartleby-objects-bags-mary-l-chan.html', 'snippet': 'Mary L Chan of Bartleby Objects has no interest in making a plain black purse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The accessories and home goods designer Mary L Chan puts it plainly: “It’s presumptuous to insist that customers are going to hand certain things down for generations.” But, she continues, “I’m also not interested in designing landfill.” She found an intersection between the too-precious and the disposable in 2014. She made her first handbag, the Trudis tote, after failing to find a carryall that satisfied her exact needs; the thought behind her subsequent leather pieces has centered on the experience of going out into the world. “What do you need to bring with you and how do you need to carry it? How do you want to present yourself?” Chan lists as considerations. “And how does the bag become a functional object that has a life of its own, and lives with everything else in your space?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/03/t-magazine/03tmag-bartleby1/03tmag-bartleby1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width'