, 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/8223cc8f-fdf3-5030-b55d-b5e8f38be497', 'word_count': 2928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8223cc8f-fdf3-5030-b55d-b5e8f38be497'}, {'abstract': 'In Afghanistan, a captain of French NATO troops and a Taliban commander are forced into an unlikely alliance by a common, invisible enemy in this film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/05/movies/neither-heaven-nor-earth-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Afghanistan, a captain of French NATO troops and a Taliban commander are forced into an unlikely alliance by a common, invisible enemy in this film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Capt. Antarès Bonassieu, the hero of Clément Cogitore’s debut feature, “Neither Heaven Nor Earth,” seems like a guy we’ve met before, at least in other war movies. Lean and muscular, with a thoughtful countenance under his blond brush cut, he is a seasoned soldier and a natural commander, tough on his men but capable of sensitivity when circumstances require it. (He is played by Jérémie Renier, a graceful chameleon who you may not recognize from Olivier Assayas’s “Summer Hours” or Jean-Pierre and Luc Dardenne’s “L’Enfant.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05NEITHER/05NEITHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05NEITHER/05NEITHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05NEITHER/05NEITHER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type