document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3956874-b018-5d5b-86c8-3b615d8774dc', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3956874-b018-5d5b-86c8-3b615d8774dc'}, {'abstract': 'New research has revealed that the plant’s internal clock and ability to detect light work together to allow the flower to face east as the sun rises and bend to the west.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/05/science/how-sunflowers-follow-the-sun-day-after-day.html', 'snippet': 'New research has revealed that the plant’s internal clock and ability to detect light work together to allow the flower to face east as the sun rises and bend to the west.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At dawn, whole fields of sunflowers stand at attention, all facing east, and begin their romance with the rising sun. As that special star appears to move across the sky, young flowers follow its light, looking up, then over and westward, catching one final glance as the sun disappears over the horizon.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/science/05tb-sunflowers01/05tb-sunflowers01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Sunflowers Follow the Sun, Day After Day', 'kicker': 'Trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sun Worshipers: The Follower Genes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunlight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biorhythms', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atamian, Hagop', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harmer, Stacey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sun', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Light', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-04T22:19:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By JoAnna Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b003d793-6ee9-562d-b67b-fd2f12c6e6b8', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b003d793-6ee9-562d-b67b-fd2f12c6e6b8'}, {'abstract': 'In an adventure story edged with unspoken risks, the young actors take the kind of chances that their more careful elders have been trained to avoid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/05/movies/little-men-review.html', 'snippet': 'In an adventure story edged with unspoken risks, the young actors take the kind of chances that their more careful elders have been trained to avoid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is hardly a shortage of buddy movies about mismatched men bonding under duress, but films that chart the emotional weather of everyday male friendship are rare. Literature has more to offer, at least as far as boys are concerned. Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn have a rich and renewable legacy. And it may be that association that imparts a novelistic vibe to Ira Sachs’s “Little Men,” beyond the Louisa May Alcott echo in the title. It’s a subtle movie, alert to the almost imperceptible currents of feeling that pass between its title characters.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05LITTLEMEN2/05LITTLEMEN2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05LITTLEMEN2/05LITTLEMEN2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05LITTLEMEN2/05LITTLEMEN2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05LITTLEMEN2/05LITTLEMEN2-articleLarge.