ubsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf1ef2a7-d7d2-547f-a0d2-68882d23a329', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf1ef2a7-d7d2-547f-a0d2-68882d23a329'}, {'abstract': 'Though other shows featured independent women, Ms. Moore’s series really stuck with viewers, during its run, from 1970 to 1977, and after.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/05/arts/television/looking-back-and-learning-from-the-mary-tyler-moore-show.html', 'snippet': 'Though other shows featured independent women, Ms. Moore’s series really stuck with viewers, during its run, from 1970 to 1977, and after.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Hillary Rodham had gone home and flipped on the TV after she delivered her Wellesley commencement address in the spring of 1969, how might she have seen the changing roles of American women reflected in popular entertainment? She could have watched Mary Tyler Moore — but it would have been in reruns of “The Dick Van Dyke Show,” where Rose Marie was the wisecracking career woman and Ms. Moore still the TV housewife, albeit the first to swap her aproned skirt for capri pants. It was not until a year after Mrs. Clinton’s classmates tossed their mortarboards that Mary Richards first tossed her tam in “The Mary Tyler Moore Show.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05FRIDAYFILE/05FRIDAYFILE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05FRIDAYFILE/05FRIDAYFILE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/05/arts/05FRIDAYFILE/05FRIDAYFILE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height':