pub_date': '2016-08-05T17:13:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Entertainment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a385a69e-c205-5095-9246-61dd52f42f93', 'word_count': 987, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a385a69e-c205-5095-9246-61dd52f42f93'}, {'abstract': 'Steeve Mackaya, a native of Gabon, sets up shop in Chelsea each morning, always decked out in a colorful, eye-catching suit, to sell newspapers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/nyregion/the-ever-so-stylish-newspaper-salesman.html', 'snippet': 'Steeve Mackaya, a native of Gabon, sets up shop in Chelsea each morning, always decked out in a colorful, eye-catching suit, to sell newspapers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steeve Mackaya arrives each morning before rush hour with three stacks of newspapers, two milk crates and, rain or shine, a different boldly colored suit. The photographer Moshe Katvan noticed him sitting in his wheelchair and was mesmerized. “He was like a piece of New York street art, totally unbelievable and amazing,” the photographer said. “You won’t see it anywhere else in the world. I asked him what he wanted, if he wanted money. He said he wanted nothing. He just wanted to say his piece.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ever-So-Stylish Newspaper Salesman', 'kicker': 'Album | MOSHE KATVAN', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worn With Pride', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gabon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Street Vendors (Non-Food)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-05T17:14:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4d15e29-d9d0-5578-932b-7b45ba0ed69d', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4d15e29-d9d0-5578-932b-7b45ba0ed69d'}, {'abstract': 'A book examines the city’s conflicted role in the Civil War, which threatened lucrative partnerships between Wall Street and the South.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/nyregion/when-new-york-leery-of-losing-business-leaned-dixie.html', 'snippet': 'A book examines the city’s conflicted role in the Civil War, which threatened lucrative partnerships between Wall Street and the South.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Because of cotton, no city in the North was more pro-South, anti-abolition or anti-Lincoln,” John Strausbaugh writes in justifying the title of his new book, “City of Sedition: The History of New York City During the Civil War” (Twelve Books, $30).', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07BOOKSHELF/07BOOKSHELF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1489, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When New York, Leery of Losing Business, Leaned Dixie', 'kicker': 'Bookshelf', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When New York Leaned Dixie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strausbaugh, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'City of Sedition: The History of New York City During the Civil War (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-05T17:17:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e33bed5-3c7c-5599-8e20-a0f01ec9c265', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e33bed5-3c7c-5599-8e20-a0f01ec9c265'}, {'abstract': 'The birds’ population plummeted in the late 1880s as a result of plume-hunting, an inspiration for the Migratory Bird Treaty Act. They are again common in summer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/nyregion/snowy-egrets-once-fashion-victims-always-elegant-predators.html', 'snippet': 'The birds’ population plummeted in the late 1880s as a result of plume-hunting, an inspiration for the Migratory Bird Treaty Act. They are again common in summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is nothing as white as a snowy egret, and few things are as graceful. Watching one hunt in the shallow waters of Hamilton Beach, Queens, one early morning, I was held rapt for almost an hour by the bird’s search for prey. Its image reflected perfectly in the dark mirror of the Hawtree Creek, it was as if two birds hunted. The scene was like a masterful Japanese print; minimal, carefully rendered and capturing an ancient truth. I pitied the school of killifish the bird had targeted.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07NATURE/07NATURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07NATURE/07NATURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/nyregion/07NATURE/0