 mineralization processes within the hackberry (Celtis occidentalis) seed.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/07jahren/07jahren-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1463, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Father’s Hackberry Tree', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Father’s Hackberry Tree', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jahren, A Hope', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laboratories and Scientific Equipment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-06T18:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hope Jahren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jahren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/37289f6d-ada5-555b-b191-4bb5e75f63e9', 'word_count': 1161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37289f6d-ada5-555b-b191-4bb5e75f63e9'}, {'abstract': 'Recent research suggests that only about half of perceived friendships are mutual.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/07/opinion/sunday/do-your-friends-actually-like-you.html', 'snippet': 'Recent research suggests that only about half of perceived friendships are mutual.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THINK of all the people with whom you interact during the course of a day, week, month and year. The many souls with whom you might exchange a greeting or give a warm embrace; engage in chitchat or have a deeper conversation. All those who, by some accident of fate, inhabit your world. And then ask yourself who among them are your friends — your true friends. Recent research indicates that only about half of perceived friendships are mutual. That is, someone you think is your friend might not be so keen on you. Or, vice versa, as when someone you feel you hardly know claims you as a bestie.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 