'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/138c1298-bbe3-577c-ab3a-bf073f286443', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/138c1298-bbe3-577c-ab3a-bf073f286443'}, {'abstract': 'At the Brooklyn Museum since 1898, it is restored and on display in Boston and Washington, D.C.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/10/dining/antinori-wine-della-robbia-terra-cotta-relief.html', 'snippet': 'At the Brooklyn Museum since 1898, it is restored and on display in Boston and Washington, D.C.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Antinoris of Florence, Italy, have been in the wine business in Tuscany since the 14th century and can trace their lineage back even further. As testimony to their importance during the Renaissance, a massive glazed terra-cotta relief, “The Resurrection of Christ,” by Giovanni della Robbia, dating from around 1524 and commissioned by the Antinoris, is a highlight of an exhibition of della Robbia works at the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston. The relief, in a half-moon shape, was created for the entrance to one of the family’s properties and includes the Antinori crest, designed by della Robbia. Since 1898 it has been in the Brooklyn Museum. The Antinoris contributed to the restoration of the work: “Della Robbia: Sculpting With Color in Renaissance Florence,” Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, to Dec. 4; National Gallery of Art, Washington, Feb. 4 to June 4, 2017.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/dining/10BURNER-ART-WEB/10BURNER-ART-WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'a