2:41:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1341da80-a7e6-5a47-a9b2-6f6b8aa9ebdc', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1341da80-a7e6-5a47-a9b2-6f6b8aa9ebdc'}, {'abstract': 'This episode’s final glimpse of Quinn and Rachel tells us that the show remembers what matters. But some serious questions remain for “UnREAL.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/08/arts/television/unreal-season-2-finale-recap.html', 'snippet': 'This episode’s final glimpse of Quinn and Rachel tells us that the show remembers what matters. But some serious questions remain for “UnREAL.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A show as meta as “UnREAL” faces the problem that often confronts satire: if you skewer a genre well enough, you might eventually become it. This season has struggled, partly by aiming for network-drama polish rather than self-aware pulp. “Everlasting” is a show about people seeking triumph amid inescapable falsity; everything in “UnREAL” echoes that, with an edge. Adding tertiary love interests and subplots just provided the season with stumbling blocks. This finale, more than anything, needed to put what mattered — Rachel, Quinn, behind-the-scenes satire, occasional heartbreak — back in the center and trust it to hold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/08/watching/08unreal/08unreal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/08/watching/08unreal/08unreal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'ra