ection_name': 'NYT Now', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adeel Hassan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adeel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hassan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/3de597ec-a8e2-517c-88eb-29a866634e55', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3de597ec-a8e2-517c-88eb-29a866634e55'}, {'abstract': 'It’s tough to be seen as a successful billionaire businessman who is also an outsider trying to work for the little guy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/09/business/dealbookemail/trumps-economic-agenda-and-the-team-behind-it.html', 'snippet': 'It’s tough to be seen as a successful billionaire businessman who is also an outsider trying to work for the little guy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Donald J. Trump has laid out his economic vision, and it included deep tax cuts for the wealthy, tearing up climate change agreements and abandoning trade deals. He insisted, “We can’t fix a rigged system by relying on the people who rigged it in the first place,” highlighting the fine line that he has to tread between between being anti-elite and yet absolutely elite. It’s tough to be seen as a successful billionaire businessman who is also an outsider trying to work for the little guy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump’s Economic Agenda and the Team Behind It', 'kicker': 'Morning Agenda', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Think Tanks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walmart Stores Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-09T09:53:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amie Tsang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tsang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Newsletter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f514a7ae-c844-5de4-9079-31a280ea7a68', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f514a7ae-c844-5de4-9079-31a280ea7a68'}, {'abstract': 'A simple trick can make it easier to stick to a budget: Insert some time between your impulse to purchase and the moment you pull out the credit card.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/10/your-money/one-secret-to-cutting-spending-wait-72-hours-before-you-buy.html', 'snippet': 'A simple trick can make it easier to stick to a budget: Insert some time between your impulse to purchase and the moment you pull out the credit card.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m going to assume for a minute that you’re one of the millions of people who have a recurrently frustrating relationship with spending. It probably starts with a commitment to sticking to your budget, and you do for a few days. Then, despite your resolve, you break down, pull out the credit card and do something that you promised you wouldn’t. You wake up in the morning ashamed and resolve never to do it again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/08/business/080816bucks-carl-sketch/080816bucks-carl-sketch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': '