', 'snippet': 'Policy in a time of fuzziness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the result of the presidential election looking relatively clear — I know, chickens not hatched and all that, but, you know, polls have actually been fairly accurate — I’m thinking more about economics. And I realized something not too flattering about myself: I’m feeling nostalgic for 2011 or so.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murky Macroeconomics', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-08-09T15:18:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99a78193-4b97-522c-aa3b-979eeec13412', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99a78193-4b97-522c-aa3b-979eeec13412'}, {'abstract': 'The hip-hop pioneers have their first new album in 12 years, “And the Anonymous Nobody.” But if you want to hear their early work, look for CDs, vinyl and cassettes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/14/arts/music/de-la-soul-digital-albums.html', 'snippet': 'The hip-hop pioneers have their first new album in 12 years, “And the Anonymous Nobody.” But if you want to hear their early work, look for CDs, vinyl and cassettes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Valentine’s Day in 2014, De La Soul did something surprising: The group gave away almost all of its work.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/arts/14DELASOUL1/14DELASOUL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_nam