mes.com/2016/08/10/opinion/hillary-clintons-economic-policies.html', 'snippet': 'A reader urges Hillary Clinton to stick with a “socialist-lite” platform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hillary Clinton’s Economic Policies', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clinton’s Economic Policies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-09T16:45:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/e31e4398-dc1f-58f4-abc1-1f99ca24cf66', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e31e4398-dc1f-58f4-abc1-1f99ca24cf66'}, {'abstract': 'The latest iteration is more nostalgic indulgence than rebellious statement. But it still has a defiant power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/09/t-magazine/fashion/gothic-lipstick-beauty.html', 'snippet': 'The latest iteration is more nostalgic indulgence than rebellious statement. But it still has a defiant power.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Insensitive to red,” a heartbreaking phrase, is technically a description of early black-and-white film, which converted the colors of blood and red lipstick to black. Elsa Lanchester’s lips were painted redder than her auburn hair for 1935’s “The Bride of Frankenstein”; the makeup artist on the film based her look on a 3,300-year-old bust of Nefertiti, who announced her power by blackening her eyes, reddening her lips and dyeing her nails with dark henna. But when Lanchester appeared on screen as the Bride — “She’s alive!” — her overdrawn mouth looked dark as hell: Et voilà, the modern gothic lip was born. Robert Smith of the Cure, whose lipstick emphasized not the Cupid’s bow but the corners of his mouth, giving him the moue of a dissatisfied cannibal, was said to inspire the almost-black “goth lip” of the 1990s; he only, in fact, wore the ’60s designer Mary Quant’s Crimson Scorcher. But what are facts when it comes to what we see? Color depends on perception, and our perception, so dependent on lighting, changes over the years the way light does over the course of a day. Sometimes we’re desensitized to red, and so to get a look as fresh as red lipstick used to give, we turn to shades that look more like ink.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '132', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0/21tmag-08look_goth-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 655}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/t-magazine/21tmag-08look_goth-t