rd.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/books/10book-cover/10book-cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/books/10book-cover/10book-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/10/books/10book-cover/10book-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 766}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/books/10book-cover/10book-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/books/10book-cover/10book-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Jay McInerney’s ‘Bright, Precious Days’', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lights Have Lost Much of Their Glow but They Still Shine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bright, Precious Days (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McInerney, Jay', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-09T21:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45d3dbc8-2e95-5655-8b59-4cf05d553060', 'word_count': 1056, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45d3dbc8-2e95-5655-8b59-4cf05d553060'}, {'abstract': 'An ambitious new restaurant in Honolulu’s still pleasantly seedy Chinatown is serving small plates that re-envision the islands’ staples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/09/t-magazine/food/senia-honolulu-restaurant-hawaii-dining.html', 'snippet': 'An ambitious new restaurant in Honolulu’s still pleasantly seedy Chinatown is serving small plates that re-envision the islands’ staples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, Hawaii’s high-end restaurants have been dominated by outsiders. At first glance, Senia, serving ambitious small plates in Honolulu’s still pleasantly seedy Chinatown, might seem like more of the same: It’s co-owned by the chef Anthony Rush, a Brit who arrived from London mere months ago. But Rush’s partner is Chris Kajioka, a Honolulu native with intimate knowledge of local ingredients. Together the chefs, who met working at Per Se, have created a menu that centers on the islands’ most elemental staples, re-envisioned as haute cuisine. Red snapper, which in Hawaii is often served simply with ginger and soy sauce, is baked in a sourdough crust and cracked tableside; backyard fruits like mountain apples and guavas are prepared with traditional French techniques; and lowly starches like taro, sweet potatoes and breadfruit are given center stage. “This is not meant to be intellectual food,” Kajioka says. “We really want to represent Hawaii — just in a different way.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '122', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/04/t-magazine/04tmag-senia/04tmag-senia-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Redefining Hawaiian Fine Dining', 'kicker': 'Food Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Redefining Hawaiian Fine Dining', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honolulu (Hawaii)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-09T21:46:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanya Yanagihara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yanagihara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd84aa14-a840-5c72-b9e9-55660231f50e', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd84aa14-a840-5c72-b9e9-55660231f50e'}, {'abstract': 'Emperor Akihito’s oblique appeal poses a problem for Japanese politicians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/10/opinion/let-the-emperor-retire.html', 'snippet': 'Emperor Akihito’s oblique appeal poses a problem for Japanese politicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It says a lot about Japan’s constitutional monarchy that Emperor Akihito, 82 and in failing health, would be stepping out of bounds if he were to say he wanted to retire. Under the Constitution laid down by the victorious United States after World War II, the emperor’s role is strictly restricted to being the “symbol of the state and of the unity of the people,” and since the law says the monarch rules until he dies, to utter words like “abdicate” or even “retire” would be seen as a forbidden sortie into politics. Yet in a televised address on Monday remarkable for its circumlocution, the emperor was clear about what he couldn’t say. And if it wasn’t clear, we’ll say it for him: He’d really, really like to retire.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/opinion/10wed3-web/10wed3-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/10/opinion/10wed3-web/10wed3-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/opinion/10wed3-web/10wed3-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit