//www.nytimes.com/2016/08/10/arts/television/mr-robot-season-2-episode-6-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Elliot’s retreat into a fantasy world is a sign of a show that believes in itself, and has a confidence that is breathtaking to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To truly appreciate how good Rami Malek is on “Mr. Robot,” you just might need to see him on the least “Mr. Robot”–like television show imaginable. Improbably, disturbingly, delightfully, that’s precisely how this week’s episode of “Mr. Robot” begins. After vintage USA Network bumpers touting the “Word Up Wednesday” programming block on “America’s Favorite TV Network” roll, we suddenly see Mr. Malek’s Elliot Alderson dropped into an 1980s family sitcom, with all the VHS picture quality and canned laughter that entails. Excised from the show’s painstaking, painterly cinematography — explicitly developed to capture Mr. Malek’s every facial nuance, right down to using curved lenses to accentuate his beautifully bulbous eyes — Elliot finally looks as out of place in the world as he feels.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/watching/10robot2/10robot2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/10/watching/10robot2/10robot2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/watching/10robot2/10robot2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/10/watching/10robot2/10robot2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 298, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/