d', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-11T09:40:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amie Tsang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tsang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08c04917-7dfc-5e24-b261-e9c0e826de40', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08c04917-7dfc-5e24-b261-e9c0e826de40'}, {'abstract': "The West's image of Islam and the Muslim image of the West are often mutually incommunicable. No area is as sensitive as the treatment of women's roles, dress and sexuality.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/12/opinion/olympians-in-hijab-and-bikini.html', 'snippet': "The West's image of Islam and the Muslim image of the West are often mutually incommunicable. No area is as sensitive as the treatment of women's roles, dress and sexuality.", 'lead_paragraph': 'Since I saw a photograph of an Egyptian and a German beach volleyball player confronting each other at the net in Rio, I have been unable to get the image out of my head. Doaa Elghobashy, aged 19, wears a hijab, long sleeves and black leggings to her ankles. Kira Walkenhorst, 25, is in a dark blue bikini. The outstretched hands of the Olympian women almost meet, the ball between them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/11/opinion/11Cohen-web/11Cohen-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 835, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/11/opinion/11Cohen-web/11C