rticle', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f489a333-fe9a-5f34-958b-8eee3b59f347', 'word_count': 1958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f489a333-fe9a-5f34-958b-8eee3b59f347'}, {'abstract': 'Programs funded by the United States are helping transform Honduras. Who says American power is dead?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/how-the-most-dangerous-place-on-earth-got-a-little-bit-safer.html', 'snippet': 'Programs funded by the United States are helping transform Honduras. Who says American power is dead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'San Pedro Sula, Honduras — Three years ago, Honduras had the highest homicide rate in the world. The city of San Pedro Sula had the highest homicide rate in the country. And the Rivera Hernández neighborhood, where 194 people were killed or hacked to death in 2013, had the highest homicide rate in the city. Tens of thousands of young Hondurans traveled to the United States to plead for asylum from the drug gangs’ violence.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/opinion/sunday/14Nazario1/14Nazario1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Most Dangerous Place on Earth Got Safer', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Longer  the Most Violent Place on Earth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nazario, Sonia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honduras', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gangs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vocational Training', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-11T12:00:56+000