ount': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ac40a0b-9141-5d04-b1cf-ed92368905a7'}, {'abstract': 'This oral history by James Andrew Miller enlists many voices in describing the rise of Creative Artists Agency and its former chairman Michael Ovitz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/12/books/review-powerhouse-recalls-a-heyday-of-matched-jaguars-and-packaged-films.html', 'snippet': 'This oral history by James Andrew Miller enlists many voices in describing the rise of Creative Artists Agency and its former chairman Michael Ovitz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Andrew Miller’s “Powerhouse” is an oral history of Creative Artists Agency, the bigfooting talent agency that was once the Tyrannosaurus rex of the entertainment industry. Right from the get-go, there are two enormous things wrong with it. The first is “oral history”: Sometimes this means a seamless narrative pieced together from the words of many speakers, but not here. In “Powerhouse,” it means a 700-page string of skips, jumps, tangents and otherwise dizzying non sequiturs.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/12/arts/12BOOKMILLER2/12BOOKMILLER2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/12/arts/12BOOKMILLER2/12BOOKMILLER2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/12/arts/12BOOKMILLER2/12BOOKMILLER2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/12/arts/12BOOKMILLER2/12BOOKMILLER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge'