title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab0b93a9-5901-5773-abfa-ea5a867908b7', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab0b93a9-5901-5773-abfa-ea5a867908b7'}, {'abstract': 'In May, the islands saw the opening of Raest, entirely dedicated to traditional Faroese fermented foods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/14/travel/raest-restaurant-torshavn-faroe-islands-denmark.html', 'snippet': 'In May, the islands saw the opening of Raest, entirely dedicated to traditional Faroese fermented foods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the era of new Nordic cuisine, diners have experienced everything from fried moss to sheep-dung smoked whiskey. But what about old Nordic cuisine? When would fermented mutton make its way to a menu again? Surprisingly, this nearly forgotten gastronomy never left the shores of the semiautonomous Faroe Islands, partly administered by Denmark. In May, the islands saw the opening of what might be the world’s first “fermented” restaurant: Raest, entirely dedicated to traditional Faroese fermented foods.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/travel/14BITESSUB/14BITESSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/14/travel/14BITESSUB/14BITESSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/travel/14BITESSUB/14BITESSUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/travel/14BITESSUB/14BITESSUB-