enerational homes continues to grow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Multigenerational Households: The Benefits, and Perils', 'kicker': 'Your Money Adviser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-12T15:21:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99a963c6-65fc-5eb3-851e-56372e1309af', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99a963c6-65fc-5eb3-851e-56372e1309af'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: America’s Holy war against the Beatles ends in August 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/12/1966-holy-war-against-beatles-ends/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: America’s Holy war against the Beatles ends in August 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America’s Holy War against the Beatles ended in Chicago on a note of humility. ‘‘I didn’t mean it. … I’m sorry I said it. I’m sorry for the mess it has made,’’ said John Lennon, making his peace with the thousands of good Christians who objected violently to his comment that the Beatles were more popular than Christ. Mr. Lennon added that he hadn’t meant to brag, but was simply ‘‘deploring’’ what seemed to be a decline of religious zeal. That was good enough for Alabama disc jockey Tommy Charles, who started a national ‘‘Ban the Beatles’’ movement after the original comment. He announced in Birmingham that he accepted the apology, and, in a grand gesture, called off the planned Birmingham bonfire of Beatles records. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Aug. 13, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1966: Holy War Against Beatles Ends', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-12T15:22:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/044d4dfc-bfe3-5571-9435-94d53d055430', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/044d4dfc-bfe3-5571-9435-94d53d055430'}, {'abstract': 'The Parkline, a new rental building in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, looms at 626 Flatbush Avenue, at Fenimore Street.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2016/08/14/realestate/standing-tall.html', 'snippet': 'The Parkline, a new rental building in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, looms at 626 Flatbush Avenue, at Fenimore Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Parkline, a new rental building in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, looms at 626 Flatbush Avenue, at Fenimore Street.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/realestate/14PROSPECT-slide-9ASH/14PROSPECT-slide-9ASH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subT