5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-13T09:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Creswell and Mary Williams Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creswell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Williams', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d24ef1f-cf37-5006-b0df-29d718613ea7', 'word_count': 1854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d24ef1f-cf37-5006-b0df-29d718613ea7'}, {'abstract': '“An African City,” the steamy Ghanaian web series about five young women looking for love, becomes a critique of both Western and African cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/14/fashion/an-african-city-sex-and-the-city.html', 'snippet': '“An African City,” the steamy Ghanaian web series about five young women looking for love, becomes a critique of both Western and African cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s get this out of the way up front: “An African City,” the steamy Ghanaian web series about five young women looking for love in Accra, is an unabashed rip-off of “Sex and the City.” There are the ubiquitous five-inch strappy sandals, the scene-stealing dresses made of Fanti fabric and the bevy of men who hop in and out of the beds of Nana Yaa, Makena, Zainab and Sade. The women are as free and liberal about sex as their American HBO foremothers, with the exception of the fifth character, Ngozi, who is such a Charlotte.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/14/fashion/14SEX1-WEB/14SEX1-WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbn