ere at Lincoln Center featured 1,000 singers and succeeded as a feel-good communal event.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/15/arts/music/review-the-public-domain-beats-the-heat-and-humidity.html', 'snippet': 'This Mostly Mozart premiere at Lincoln Center featured 1,000 singers and succeeded as a feel-good communal event.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the actual temperature hovered in the mid-90s late Saturday afternoon in New York, the “feels like” indicator on weather sites put the figure at 105 degrees. Even so, the premiere of David Lang’s “the public domain,” his new work for 1,000 voices, went on as planned in the main plaza of Lincoln Center. Arriving for the performance, I was certain that the volunteer choristers who had rehearsed the piece so thoroughly would show up. But would anyone else?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15KAHANE1/15KAHANE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1348, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘The public domain’ Beats the Heat and Humidity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Heat and Humidity? They’re No Match for This Vocal Army', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mostly Mozart Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lang, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kahane, Jeffrey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnatan, Inon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-14T21:15:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da958bd-4b99-56e0-bf5d-e5ee33f0973e', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da958bd-4b99-56e0-bf5d-e5ee33f0973e'}, {'abstract': 'This book by Ben Greenman, a collection of essays, encompasses a range of music genres as the author riffs on a number of topics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/15/books/review-emotional-rescue-ruminates-on-life-and-music.html', 'snippet': 'This book by Ben Greenman, a collection of essays, encompasses a range of music genres as the author riffs on a number of topics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They strode upon the land like gods. They had formidable egos and captivating prose styles and strong, often unpredictable opinions. They had influence, too, at least for a while, and maybe even a modicum of power. I speak here of the rock critics — writers like Lester Bangs, Robert Christgau, Ellen Willis and Dave Marsh — who rose to prominence in the 1960s and ’70s, and I mention them as a way of providing a contrast to Ben Greenman’s new book, “Emotional Rescue.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKGREENMAN2/15BOOKGREENMAN2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKGREENMAN2/15BOOKGREENMAN2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 845}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKGREENMAN2/15BOOKGREENMAN2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 728, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKGREENMAN2/15BOOKGREENMAN2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 967, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKGREENMAN2/15BOOKGREENMAN2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/15/arts/15BOOKG