ng-bearded owner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO — After a long day trying to set world records, win a medal or just finish a strenuous event, even the world’s top athletes want to unwind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/17/world/americas/17rio-web/17binladen-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Olympic Athletes Unwind in Rio: The Bin Laden Bar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2016)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-16T15:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a12dc49-e08c-5a14-ab5d-ec08eb0fabdc', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a12dc49-e08c-5a14-ab5d-ec08eb0fabdc'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: Comtesse Potocka steals dogs in Paris in August 1891.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/16/1891-a-dogomaniac-near-paris/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: Comtesse Potocka steals dogs in Paris in August 1891.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Comtesse Potocka, a Polish lady, living at St. Cloud, has a mania for stealing dogs. She lives quite alone and the neighbors were for a long time annoyed by the noises and smells which proceeded from her dog-haunted premises. At last her neglect to pay her rent gave the police an opportunity of forcing their way into the house. In the various rooms and the garden there were found forty-one dogs, some tied to trees, others shut up in rooms. As their meals had been regulated more by the good humor of the Comtesse than by their own natural cravings, some of the animals were in a painful condition, and had to be shot. — The New York Herald, European Edition, August 17, 1891', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1891: A Dogomaniac Near Paris', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-16T15:32:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77fc24e7-d06c-5d03-813c-106fe1bf0729', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77fc24e7-d06c-5d03-813c-106fe1bf0729'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: An Iraqi Air Force captain defects to Israel in August 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/16/1966-iraqi-defects-to-israel/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of The International Herald Tribune: An Iraqi Air Force captain defects to Israel in August 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEL AVIV — An Iraqi Air Force captain defected to Israel today [Aug. 16] in his Russian-built MiG-21 supersonic jet fighter. Israeli military officers here said it was the first time a MiG-21 had been deposited intact in Western hands. The 30-year-old pilot said he was a Catholic, and that religious discrimination had affected his chances for promotion. He said that he began planning his defection 18 months ago, and had decided to fly to Israel because that was the only country within flying range which would not hand him back to Iraq. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Aug. 17, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1966: Iraqi Defects to Israel', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'su