sh psychedelia of the late 1960s and early ’70s — Tim Buckley, John Martyn, Nick Drake — with a coy precision that probably went a little too far. From the title to the cover illustration, it sold a convenient fiction: the idea that Mr. Walker was a torchbearer, rather than a trickster.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/18/arts/18ALBUM2/18ALBUM2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Ryley Walker Is Unsettled in a Chicago Way on ‘Golden Sings’', 'kicker': 'Album Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Age-Old Tales New Again, Free From Any Strict Genre or Style', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Ryley (1989- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Golden Sings That Have Been Sung (Album)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-17T20:46:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33db4c59-fa7c-57bd-8b62-38d783b41906', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33db4c59-fa7c-57bd-8b62-38d783b41906'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Modern Love Podcast is an encore episode for prom season, featuring the “Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt” actor reading the story of a gay high school student who gets invited to the big event again and again and again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/17/fashion/tituss-burgess-modern-love-podcast.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s Modern Love Podcast is an encore episode for prom season, featuring the “Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt” actor reading the story of a gay high school student who gets invited to the big event again and again and again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this encore episode of Modern Love: The Podcast, the actor Tituss Burgess (“Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt,” “30 Rock”) reads the essay “A Prince Charming for the Prom (Not Ever After, Though),” about a gay student who forgoes the prom of his dreams to help his female friends realize theirs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2005/05/29/fashion/29MODERNLOVE/29MODERNLOVE-thu