dlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bui', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18757514-8d13-5b80-a554-21d481d4c623', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18757514-8d13-5b80-a554-21d481d4c623'}, {'abstract': 'The portraiture of William Eggleston, whose color photography helped shepherd the medium into the art world, is the exclusive feature of a new exhibit and book.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/18/highly-personal-portraiture-by-william-eggleston/', 'snippet': 'The portraiture of William Eggleston, whose color photography helped shepherd the medium into the art world, is the exclusive feature of a new exhibit and book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most portrait artists attempt to differentiate the emotional essence of each individual subject. But William Eggleston, a father of color photography whose 1976 Museum of Modern Art exhibit arranged by John Szarkowski helped shepherd the medium into the art world — much to the chagrin of critics — insists that he photographs a person the same way he photographs a parking lot. A new exhibition of his portraiture at the National Portrait Gallery in London, however, belies that assertion by displaying more than 100 highly personal images. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For William Eggleston, People Are Like Parking Lots', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eggleston, William (1939- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Portrait Gallery (London)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T08:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rena Silverman