Games (2016)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarey, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decathlon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eaton, Ashton', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T13:54:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26e19dac-c460-5c2b-851c-5f1a161579c7', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26e19dac-c460-5c2b-851c-5f1a161579c7'}, {'abstract': '“The Secret Life of Textiles: Animal Fibers” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art also gets into the often inscrutable facts of textiles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/arts/design/delving-into-the-history-and-beauty-of-textiles-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': '“The Secret Life of Textiles: Animal Fibers” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art also gets into the often inscrutable facts of textiles.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Secret Life of Textiles: Animal Fibers” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art delves deep into the history and beauty but also the often inscrutable facts of textiles. Its two dozen items range across several cultures and centuries, including Chinese textile fragments from the fourth century B.C., Indian and Russian shawls, men’s hats from Tibet and Korea, and a 19th-century French Jacquard-weave portrait of George Washington. Building on nature’s genius, these often labor-intensive artifacts use fibers from yaks, sheep, goats, horses, cows and silkworms. One of the few intact garments in this show is a lavish ceremonial robe from 19th-century China discreetly trimmed in cat fur. Especially enlightening are the works’ labels, which parse the different fibers’ morphologies and the techniques that transformed them, aided by greatly magnified photographs. (Through Feb. 20; 212-535-7710, metmuseum.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE5/21GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Delving Into the History and Beauty of Textiles at the Met', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Learning History, Fiber by Fiber', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Secret Life of Textiles: Animal Fibers (Exhibit)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T13:54:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fe2bf34-731d-5dc5-ae14-4aa39096b75f', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fe2bf34-731d-5dc5-ae14-4aa39096b75f'}, {'abstract': 'Among the performers are the pianist Randy Weston, the singer Jazzmeia Horn and the alto saxophonist Grace Kelly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/arts/music/charlie-parker-jazz-festival-has-free-shows-for-many-tastes.html', 'snippet': 'Among the performers are the pianist Randy Weston, the singer Jazzmeia Horn and the alto saxophonist Grace Kelly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Charlie Parker Jazz Festival is one of the more generous rites of summer in the city: a weekend’s worth of free performances, uptown and downtown, with a lineup that cuts across age and style. Named after a peerless alto saxophonist and bebop lodestar, the festival usually spotlights one of his former contemporaries. This year, it’s the magisterial pianist Randy Weston, who recently turned 90; he is to perform on Saturday, Aug. 27, with his African Rhythms Sextet at Marcus Garvey Park.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE3/21GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1431, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charlie Parker Jazz Festival Has Free Shows for Many Tastes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Free Listening: Piano, Sax, Drums', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parker, Charlie, Jazz Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weston, Randy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McCaslin, Donny, Quartet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T13:56:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7b146e7-2711-5ca8-94de-bacf30a6d3bf', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7b146e7-2711-5ca8-94de-bacf30a6d3bf'}, {'abstract': 'Why we’re retiring the app, and what you can expect next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/18/nytnow/a-note-from-the-editors-of-nyt-now.html', 'snippet': 'Why we’re retiring the app, and what you can expect next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear NYT Now reader,', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Note From the Editors of NYT Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T13:57:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'NYT Now', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df0f996-518c-5a8c-b73e-2ee3ca21b795', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df0f996-518c-5a8c-b73e-2ee3ca21b795'}, {'abstract': 'Programs at Merkin Concert Hall and the Kaplan Penthouse provide divergent listening opportunities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/arts/music/exploring-jesus-and-josephine-baker-through-music.html', 'snippet': 'Programs at Merkin Concert Hall and the Kaplan Penthouse provide divergent listening opportunities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1680, the Baroque composer and organist Dietrich Buxtehude wrote “Membra Jesu Nostri,” a cycle of seven cantatas reflecting on the body of Christ. Over two programs on Sunday, Aug. 21, at Merkin Concert Hall, the International Contemporary Ensemble (ICE), in residence this summer at the Mostly Mozart Festival, will perform the cantatas along with seven works written in response to them by a diverse group of composers. The new pieces will be introduced in two groups: The first, at 3 p.m., will feature works by David T. Little, Anna Thorvaldsdottir, Caroline Shaw and Hans Thomalla; for the second, at 7 p.m., Buxtehude’s sacred cantatas will be heard along with pieces by Lewis Spratlan, Pelle Gudmundsen-Holmgreen and Santa Ratniece. The Crossing, an acclaimed chamber choir, joins ICE for these bold programs.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 810, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 810}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 759, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE1/21GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1275, 'width': 944, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Jesus and Josephine Baker Through Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical; Sharing Some Heavenly Sounds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mostly Mozart Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buxtehude, Dieterich (1637-1707)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Contemporary Ensemble', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bullock, Julia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Josephine (1906-75)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T13:59:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2456d079-ec24-5dfe-a559-ff3b763f9f7f', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2456d079-ec24-5dfe-a559-ff3b763f9f7f'}, {'abstract': 'The app, a curated list of stories intended to broaden The Times’s reader base, will no longer be available to download starting the week of Aug. 29.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/business/media/new-york-times-to-shelve-nyt-now-app.html', 'snippet': 'The app, a curated list of stories intended to broaden The Times’s reader base, will no longer be available to download starting the week of Aug. 29.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In early 2014, The New York Times introduced an app called NYT Now that would provide a curated list of stories — for a lower price than a full digital subscription — to what it hoped would be a younger, mobile-savvy audience. Jill Abramson, then the executive editor of The Times, unveiled the app at the South by Southwest conference in Austin, Tex.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Times to Shelve NYT Now App', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Times Shuts Down the Curated News App NYT Now', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef141fae-06e4-574a-8cfe-626badaf3adc', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef141fae-06e4-574a-8cfe-626badaf3adc'}, {'abstract': 'Ellen McLaughlin, who played the celestial messenger in “Angels in America” on Broadway, brings her “Trojan Women” to the Flea Theater’s resident actors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/theater/an-angel-introduces-bats-to-trojans.html', 'snippet': 'Ellen McLaughlin, who played the celestial messenger in “Angels in America” on Broadway, brings her “Trojan Women” to the Flea Theater’s resident actors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Greek tragedians, the dramatist Ellen McLaughlin writes, “ask the hardest questions.” Perhaps best known for portraying the celestial messenger in the Broadway production of Tony Kushner’s “Angels in America,” Ms. McLaughlin continues to ask those questions from her own perspective, in her own language. Having refracted the Hellenic take on the nature of humanity and mortality, on war and its aftermath, in contemporary adaptations of works by Sophocles (“Oedipus”) and Aeschylus (“The Persians”), Ms. McLaughlin revisits the battle-ravaged plains and psyches of Euripides’ all too timely “The Trojan Women,” at the Flea Theater. Directed by Anne Cecelia Haney, with choreography by Joya Powell, the show begins performances on Wednesday, Aug. 24, featuring the Flea’s resident acting company, the Bats. (Through Sept. 26; 866-811-4111, theflea.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE2/21GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Angel Introduces Bats to Trojans', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater; Angel Introduces Bats to Trojans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Trojan Women (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bats, The (Theater Co)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Flea Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLaughlin, Ellen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haney, Anne Cecelia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9312e5a4-57e2-5653-aef8-c2e94bd94d52', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9312e5a4-57e2-5653-aef8-c2e94bd94d52'}, {'abstract': 'The Qatari-American artist Sophia Al-Maria presents a dystopian view of the Gulf states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/arts/design/black-friday-shares-sophia-al-marias-vision-of-gulf-futurism.html', 'snippet': 'The Qatari-American artist Sophia Al-Maria presents a dystopian view of the Gulf states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At art school in London a decade or so ago, the Qatari-American artist, writer and filmmaker Sophia Al-Maria came up with a pithy term, Gulf Futurism, to describe the warp-speed transformations of Dubai and other oil-rich cities: the rise of hotels, malls and museums and the incorporation of the area’s Bedouin tribes into an international consumer class. She has been elaborating on this catchphrase ever since, in videos and writings that combine sci-fi fantasies with dystopian musings on the human and environmental costs of hyperdevelopment.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1308, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19ALMARI-web/19ALMARI-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Luxury Mall as Consumer Prison', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Luxury Mall as Consumer Prison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Al-Maria, Sophia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04f42694-9f74-5249-854b-48b6a8539b18', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04f42694-9f74-5249-854b-48b6a8539b18'}, {'abstract': '“History of the World, Part I” is one of seven Brooks movies being served with food and drinks at the theater-restaurant Syndicated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/movies/a-week-of-mel-brooks-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': '“History of the World, Part I” is one of seven Brooks movies being served with food and drinks at the theater-restaurant Syndicated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s moronic, tasteless and celebrating its 35th anniversary this year? “History of the World, Part I,” Mel Brooks’s 1981 cameo-filled collection of ersatz historical episodes. Janet Maslin, reviewing the film when it came out, complained that “many of the jokes are either meanspirited or scatological, or both,” and these days the best way to watch it may be with an accompaniment of grub and alcoholic beverages. That’s possible Monday, Aug. 22, at Syndicated, the theater and restaurant in Bushwick, Brooklyn, which begins a Brooks week with the movie that evening. Sample gag: Moses comes down from the mountain carrying three tablets and announces to his people, “The Lord Jehovah has given unto you these 15 [drops one tablet, which shatters] — 10! Ten Commandments.” The rest of the week’s lineup: “Blazing Saddles,” “Robin Hood: Men in Tights,” “Spaceballs,” “Young Frankenstein,” “The Producers” and “High Anxiety.” (syndicatedbk.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 295}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/arts/21GUIDE6/21GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1460, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Week of Mel Brooks in Brooklyn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film; All the Broken Commandments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syndicated (Brooklyn, NY, Bar, Restaurant, Movie Theater)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'History of the World, Part I (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Mel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:02:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/541dea55-9264-5934-ad72-a93e300f52b3', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/541dea55-9264-5934-ad72-a93e300f52b3'}, {'abstract': 'Istalif’s resurgence after the fall of the Taliban in 2001 hardened the reputation for resilience it earned over centuries, but now it is threatened again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/world/asia/afghanistan-istalif-pottery.html', 'snippet': 'Istalif’s resurgence after the fall of the Taliban in 2001 hardened the reputation for resilience it earned over centuries, but now it is threatened again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTALIF, Afghanistan — To survive the last catastrophe, when their town was sacked and burned by the Taliban, the potters of the northern Afghan town of Istalif buried the tools of their trade and fled, some enduring years of exile until the threat finally passed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/16/world/16AFGHANPOTTERY1/16AFGHANPOTTERY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'War and Pillaging Couldn’t Break an Afghan Village, but a Tumbling Economy May', 'kicker': 'Istalif Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'War Couldn’t Break Afghan Village, but a Poor Economy May', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istalif (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:10:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b2b9900-fd02-59b7-9667-bb615c5f823d', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b2b9900-fd02-59b7-9667-bb615c5f823d'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: Britain stops exports to Sweden in August 1916.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/18/1916-britain-stops-exports-to-sweden/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: Britain stops exports to Sweden in August 1916.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON, England — The King to-day [Aug. 18] signed the proclamation forbidding all exports from Great Britain to Sweden, except under special license. General satisfaction is expressed in the press at the energetic measures taken to prevent the re-exportation of merchandise from Sweden to enemy countries. It is fully realized here that the Government has been forced to action by sheer force of circumstances, of which the Swedish Trade Law of April 16, 1916, making it illegal for an importer in Sweden to furnish an exporter in the United Kingdom with information regarding the disposal of the goods, was the climax. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1916: Britain Stops Exports to Sweden', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'GREAT BRITAIN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SWEDEN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T14:20:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51010a1f-3f14-5012-9f35-449e75ebf7bd', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51010a1f-3f14-5012-9f35-449e75ebf7bd'}, {'abstract': 'Intel announced Alloy, a platform for virtual reality; and Cisco Systems, a maker of networking equipment, said it would cut about 5,500 jobs as it focuses on software.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/technology/daily-report-intel-and-cisco-shifting-with-the-times.html', 'snippet': 'Intel announced Alloy, a platform for virtual reality; and Cisco Systems, a maker of networking equipment, said it would cut about 5,500 jobs as it focuses on software.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Technology companies have to adapt continually to shifting trends. That was abundantly clear this week when two longtime tech stalwarts made moves to change with the times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/technology/19bitsdaily-web/19bitsdaily-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/19/technology/19bitsdaily-web/19bitsdaily-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/technology/19bitsdaily-web/19bitsdaily-w