 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T17:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e714d881-10f0-585f-9360-cc01c8d8e79f', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e714d881-10f0-585f-9360-cc01c8d8e79f'}, {'abstract': 'The glazed terra-cotta technique that became a specialty is showcased in “Della Robbia: Sculpting With Color in Renaissance Florence” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/arts/design/the-art-of-della-robbia-from-earth-and-water-pure-beauty.html', 'snippet': 'The glazed terra-cotta technique that became a specialty is showcased in “Della Robbia: Sculpting With Color in Renaissance Florence” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — In Renaissance Italy, art was a form of advertising, pushing hopes and emotions as much as things. Allegorical images — Temperance, Justice, Charity — on urban facades promoted civic pride and obedience. In churches, sculptural angels offered getaway deals to heaven. Home icons of the Virgin and Child taught that salvation lay not in riches but in fruitfulness, patience and a good-night kiss.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19DELLAROBBIAjp1/19DELLAROBBIAjp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19DELLAROBBIAjp1/19DELLAROBBIAjp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'th