rt or Steven Tyler, Franz Nicolay refers to Montaigne, Tocqueville, V.S. Pritchett, Ford Madox Ford and Melville — and that’s just in a two-page stretch of the introduction to his new travelogue, “The Humorless \xadLadies of Border Control.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/books/review/0821-BKS-OpenBook/0821-BKS-OpenBook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Outside the Bars', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outside The Bars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nicolay, Franz (1977- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Humorless Ladies of Border Control (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T20:12:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f798ca0c-e72e-5823-88e2-1ed4fe9554d7', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f798ca0c-e72e-5823-88e2-1ed4fe9554d7'}, {'abstract': 'Works by the doomed Polish-Jewish artist Moshe Rynecki inspired his great-granddaughter Elizabeth Rynecki to write a book and make a documentary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/arts/design/a-moral-imperative-to-recover-a-lost-art-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'Works by the doomed Polish-Jewish artist Moshe Rynecki inspired his great-granddaughter Elizabeth Rynecki to write a book and make a documentary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last time anyone is known to have heard from the Polish-Jewish artist Moshe Rynecki was in 1943, when his wife, Perla, received a postcard that he had sent from Majdanek, a concentration camp near Lublin, Poland. The Nazis had transported him there from the smoldering ruins of the Jewish ghetto in Warsaw. The rest of his family had stayed in Warsaw, posing as Christians. His scrawled message reassured his wife that he was well and “painting in the camp,” his son, George Rynecki, recalled in a memoir written decades after the war.', 'print_section': 'C', 'prin