ginal': 'By Carol Giacomo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giacomo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dd4cee0-61f4-54c7-a489-03dd343c8c16', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dd4cee0-61f4-54c7-a489-03dd343c8c16'}, {'abstract': 'The film follows the West German prosecutor whose efforts to track down fugitive Nazi war criminals was often obstructed by superiors and underlings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/movies/the-people-vs-fritz-bauer-review.html', 'snippet': 'The film follows the West German prosecutor whose efforts to track down fugitive Nazi war criminals was often obstructed by superiors and underlings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fritz Bauer is a mass, and a mess, of contradictions: crafty yet principled, loyal yet friendless, brooding yet driven. Sometimes he charges ahead, other times he bumbles along, usually engulfed in a cloud of tobacco smoke. Burghart Klaussner, playing him in “The People vs. Fritz Bauer,” delivers a masterly performance as this complicated man.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1274, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19PEOPLEVS/19PEOPLEVS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Treasonous Play for Justice in ‘The People vs. Fritz Bauer’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tracking Nazi Criminals, With an Eye on Eichmann', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kraume, Lars (1973- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klaussner, Burghart (1949- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-18T21:17:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e03d7354-0978-5a0e-bd3b-ddf0c8b53e3d', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e03d7354-0978-5a0e-bd3b-ddf0c8b53e3d'}, {'abstract': 'Chronicling the tensions facing a Korean-American teenager, Andrew Ahn’s first feature contemplates the anxieties of outsiders trying to find their place in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/19/movies/spa-night-review.html', 'snippet': 'Chronicling the tensions facing a Korean-American teenager, Andrew Ahn’s first feature contemplates the anxieties of outsiders trying to find their place in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because David Cho (Joe Seo), the glum, bashful 18-year-old protagonist of “Spa Night,” is struggling with homosexual desire, it would be easy to categorize this cool, exquisitely observant first feature by the Korean-American filmmaker Andrew Ahn as a gay movie. But it is much more. The film is a contemplation of the loneliness, tension and anxiety of outsiders pursuing a piece of the American dream.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 589, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1173, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/19/arts/19SPANIGHT/19SPANIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credi