': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-20T18:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/de6c19fc-831c-545d-b6ad-03410192a489', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de6c19fc-831c-545d-b6ad-03410192a489'}, {'abstract': 'In Brazil, many of us are so obsessed with how the outside world sees us that we won’t recognize our own faults.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/21/opinion/sunday/i-criticized-the-olympics-that-doesnt-make-me-a-traitor.html', 'snippet': 'In Brazil, many of us are so obsessed with how the outside world sees us that we won’t recognize our own faults.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO — I thought of beginning this column by praising Brazil’s friendly and open-minded people, the natural beauty of our landscape and the supreme delight that are Globo biscuits. But perhaps sarcasm isn’t the best response to those who blame me for writing only bad things about my country, ruining the image of our beloved land of samba and caipirinhas.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/opinion/sunday/21BARBARA/21BARBARA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/21/opinion/sunday/21BARBARA/21BARBARA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/21/opinion/sunday/21BARBARA/21BARBARA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 