 {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garner, Eric', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Michael E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-22T14:48:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niraj Chokshi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niraj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chokshi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b994d28-1d1c-5ef5-ab30-8a2aafd832fc', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b994d28-1d1c-5ef5-ab30-8a2aafd832fc'}, {'abstract': 'This amber spirit is made from chiles that are not quite ripe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/24/dining/chile-liqueur-ancho-reyes-verde.html', 'snippet': 'This amber spirit is made from chiles that are not quite ripe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Add a soupçon of intrigue to your next cocktail or glass of sparkling wine with a splash of poblano green chile liqueur from Mexico. It’s the new sibling of Ancho Reyes Chile Liqueur, and delivers a clean, deeply vegetal aroma and sweet but not punishing heat. The green bottle is deceptive because the spirit itself is light amber. It’s made from chiles that are less ripe than the ones used in the more toasty Ancho Reyes, a spirit that was introduced in 2014 after being developed from a Puebla recipe dating from 1927. The new version will transform a margarita: Ancho Reyes Verde Chile Liqueur, $35.99 at Astor Wines & Spirits, astorwines.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/dining/24BURNER-LIQUEUR-WEB/24BURNER-LIQUEUR-WEB-thumbStanda