ne': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/59c886e2-994a-5fe5-8849-2d3495d86f2e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/59c886e2-994a-5fe5-8849-2d3495d86f2e'}, {'abstract': 'Campaign-trail blunders allow reporters to criticize politicians without criticizing what they stand for. But what happens when a candidate says exactly what he thinks?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/magazine/how-donald-trump-blew-up-the-gaffe.html', 'snippet': 'Campaign-trail blunders allow reporters to criticize politicians without criticizing what they stand for. But what happens when a candidate says exactly what he thinks?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a politician says something stupid, reporters are traditionally expected to mask their glee with a veneer of shock and disappointment. It was in this mode, more or less, that the NBC News political director, Chuck Todd, and his colleague Carrie Dann began the Aug. 3 edition of First Read, the network’s daily political tip sheet, warning that Donald Trump’s campaign had gone “from ‘unraveling’ to ‘break glass’ mode.” They began with a heroic attempt to catalog all the previously inconceivable-for-a-presidential-nominee things Trump said and did in the preceding 24 hours, a long list that included the candidate’s displaying to a rally audience the Purple Heart medal he had been given by a retired U.S. Army lieutenant colonel who was wounded in Iraq. “I always wanted to get the Purple Heart,” said Trump, who received draft deferments during the Vietnam War for, among other things, bone spurs that had miraculously little impact on his youthful athletic career. “This was much easier.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28firstwords-copy/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1802, 'width': 1802, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Donald Trump Blew Up the ‘Gaffe’', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Say Anything', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Homans, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-23T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Homans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Homans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f049df9-220f-5b12-9d3e-4940401b47e5', 'word_count': 1451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f049df9-220f-5b12-9d3e-4940401b47e5'}, {'abstract': 'As capital punishment declines nationwide, a tiny fraction of the country generates an alarming number of death sentences. What this new geography tells us about justice in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/magazine/where-the-death-penalty-still-lives.html', 'snippet': 'As capital punishment declines nationwide, a tiny fraction of the country generates an alarming number of death sentences. What this new geography tells us about justice in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a Saturday evening in July 2013, just before 6:30, James Rhodes was recorded on a surveillance camera walking into a Metro PCS cellphone store in Jacksonville, Fla. He was wearing a black do-rag and a blue bandanna, which he pulled over his nose and mouth. Shelby Farah, the store manager, stood behind the counter. Rhodes pointed a gun at her and demanded the money in the cash register. Shelby gave it to him. Then Rhodes shot her in the head. She was 20 years old. He was 21.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28mag-28death-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28mag-28death-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28mag-28death-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28mag-28death-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28death5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28death5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/magazine/28death5/28death5-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where the Death Penalty Still Lives', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '16 Counties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farah, Shelby (1993-2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bazelon, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rhodes, James Xavier', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corey, Angela B', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breyer, Stephen G', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jacksonville (Fla)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-23T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc043eeb-ab75-549c-af97-a6abf5482929', 'word_count': 6637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc043eeb-ab75-549c-af97-a6abf5482929'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Obama met with residents on a trip that required a delicate mix of compassion and politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/24/us/politics/obama-louisiana-floods.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Obama met with residents on a trip that required a delicate mix of compassion and politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZACHARY, La. — For Linda Fernandez, this summer has been one long disaster tour. In July, her daughter died in Baton Rouge, while her infant great-granddaughter mysteriously ailed in Houston. It was at a hospital there, a week and a half ago, where Ms. Fernandez learned she had lost her home, too, to flooding.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Visits Louisiana Flood Victims, but Some Are Busy Just Hanging On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Visits Victims of Flooding, but Some Are Busy Just Hanging On', 'name': N