': '2016-08-24T07:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Grinspan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grinspan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/965295a5-2fc5-5e4d-8af4-2ff571dc811d', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/965295a5-2fc5-5e4d-8af4-2ff571dc811d'}, {'abstract': 'The episode exposed serious flaws at all levels of airport security.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/24/opinion/pandemonium-at-jfk-airport.html', 'snippet': 'The episode exposed serious flaws at all levels of airport security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If law enforcement officials wanted to stage a mock terrorist attack to test security at New York City’s Kennedy International Airport, they couldn’t have planned it better than what actually happened on Aug. 14. That Sunday evening passengers at the huge network of terminals suddenly began hearing noises, followed shortly by reports — false, it turned out — of gunfire.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/24wed3web/24wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/24wed3web/24wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/24wed3web/24wed3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/24wed3web/24wed3web-superJumbo.jp