e, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/nyregion/24NYTODAY1/24NYTODAY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/24/nyregion/24NYTODAY1/24NYTODAY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/nyregion/24NYTODAY1/24NYTODAY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Today: When War Came to Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'New York Today', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-08-24T10:05:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Wolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/40fed5d3-e945-52ac-873b-9279ec40a2eb', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40fed5d3-e945-52ac-873b-9279ec40a2eb'}, {'abstract': 'India’s film industry reflects the global South’s troubling transformations in a way few other art forms can.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/25/opinion/why-do-i-love-bollywood.html', 'snippet': 'India’s film industry reflects the global South’s troubling transformations in a way few other art forms can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a burning-hot afternoon last month, I found myself bawling my guts out in a Times Square movie theater. I was watching “Sultan,” a nearly three-hour-long Bollywood extravaganza that was released in July and is already one of the highest grossing Indian films ever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/opinion/aatish-taseer/aatish-taseer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Do I Love Bollywood?', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why I love Bollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-24T10:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aatish Taseer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aatish', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taseer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/77d738e3-40cd-5a5d-959f-d079e2983125', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77d738e3-40cd-5a5d-959f-d079e2983125'}, {'abstract': 'One of the growing pains of Malaysia’s economic prosperity is that its capital’s traffic problem is reaching critical mass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/25/opinion/gridlock-comes-to-kuala-lumpur.html', 'snippet': 'One of the growing pains of Malaysia’s economic prosperity is that its capital’s traffic problem is reaching critical mass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ostrich is as tall as the cars around it, and running at a fair pace in the fast lane of the Federal Highway, which links Kuala Lumpur’s city center to Petaling Jaya, its largest satellite town. As the video of the surreal incident — the result of a tame ostrich’s escaping from captivity — circulated on social media in June, what struck many observers was that the giant bird was able to run so freely along the busiest of the capital’s many traffic-clogged highways. An hour or two later and the adventurous ostrich would have been hemmed in by gridlocked traffic, just like the rest of us.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1560, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/opinion/edaw-2/edaw-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gridlock Comes to Kuala Lumpur', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gridlock Comes to Kuala Lumpur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kuala Lumpur (Malaysia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-24T10:55:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tash Aw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tash', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dcf2f04-ac14-561f-8b16-0f7f9d9b14c0', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dcf2f04-ac14-561f-8b16-0f7f9d9b14c0'}, {'abstract': 'A new study shows how the American military failed at the soldier’s most basic responsibility: keeping track of firearms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/23/magazine/how-many-guns-did-the-us-lose-track-of-in-iraq-and-afghanistan-hundreds-of-thousands.html', 'snippet': 'A new study shows how the American military failed at the soldier’s most basic responsibility: keeping track of firearms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early this year, a Facebook user in Baghdad using the name Hussein Mahyawi posted a photograph of a slightly worn M4 assault rifle he was offering for sale. Veterans of the latest war in Iraq immediately recognized it. It was a standard American carbine equipped with a holographic sight, a foregrip that was military-issue during the occupation and a sticker bearing a digital QR code used by American forces for inventory control. Except for one detail — an aftermarket pistol grip, the sort of accessory with which combatants of the current generation often pimp their guns — it was a dead ringer for any of the tens of thousands of M4s the Pentagon handed out to Iraqi security forces and various armed militias after toppling Saddam Hussein in 2003. And here it was on the open market, ready for bids.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/23/magazine/23mag-guns/23mag-guns-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/23/magazine/23mag-guns/23mag-guns-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/23/magazine/23mag-guns/23mag-guns-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/23/magazine/23mag-guns/23mag-guns-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1298, 'width': 2048, 'subType'