Jumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/25/technology/personaltech/25techtipwebART/25techtipwebART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/25/technology/personaltech/25techtipwebART/25techtipwebART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/25/technology/personaltech/25techtipwebART/25techtipwebART-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Get Twitter’s Blue Badge of Authenticity', 'kicker': 'Tech Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-24T15:01:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/943b6bda-6e58-5c8b-bf10-d1fba3054bdd', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/943b6bda-6e58-5c8b-bf10-d1fba3054bdd'}, {'abstract': '“Eat the World With Emeril Lagasse,” a six-episode Amazon series, debuts on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/arts/television/emeril-lagasse-eat-the-world-amazon.html', 'snippet': '“Eat the World With Emeril Lagasse,” a six-episode Amazon series, debuts on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How does a chef divine the connection between food and place? He asks another chef, naturally. In “Eat the World With Emeril Lagasse,” a six-episode Amazon series that begins Friday, Sept. 2, Mr. Lagasse (of Food Network fame) nibbles his way around the globe with guidance from some well-traveled colleagues. In China, Mario Batali joins him on a quest for the best dumpling soup. In Sweden, the teacher is Marcus Samuelsson, and the lesson is New Nordic cuisine. Danny Bowien provides an introduction to Jeong Kwan, a Zen Buddhist nun in South Korea known as the Philosopher Chef. And José Andrés takes Mr. Lagasse to Barcelona, where the modernist genius Ferran Adrià and his army of thinkers dissect food down to the molecule. It might be Amazon’s answer to Netflix’s “Chef’s Table,” with enough food porn and zesty banter to keep viewers aroused. But fears of a loud American prowling the globe should be laid to rest: Not a single “bam!” resounded in the two episodes I watched.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/arts/28GUIDE5/28GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/28/arts/28GUIDE5/28GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/arts/28GUIDE5/28GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/arts/28GUIDE5/28GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1267, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 