fumi Yamashita never imagined he would cultivate produce for a living. He was an expert in the Japanese tradition of bonsai when he moved from Tokyo to Paris almost 30 years ago. Asked by a chef to grow vegetables unavailable in local markets, he went back to Japan to look for seeds: “The quality there makes them more potent and reliable,” he says. But it’s his careful tending that has made him the designated grower for four Michelin-starred restaurants in Paris, including L’Astrance and Le Cinq. “I know each of my crops because I listen to them,” he explains. “Some years, my turnips decide to taste like pears, some years they taste like parsnips.” Yamashita is a key player in a revolution in French cuisine, what he describes as “taking the vegetable away from its role as a side and making it the key element on the plate.” But not just anyone is worthy of his produce; he handpicks the chefs he supplies to be sure they respect his offerings. “Giving my vegetables away is like marrying off my daughter.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/t-magazine/Yamashita-slide-EW70/Yamashita-slide-EW70-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/24/t-magazine/Yamashita-slide-EW70/Yamashita-slide-EW70-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/t-magazine/Yamashita-slide-EW70/Yamashita-slide-EW70-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/24/t-magazine/Yamashita-slide-EW70/Yamashita-slide-EW70-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlar