, 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/5c6579b5-68f3-5b30-b5e4-f2553a9d2491', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/5c6579b5-68f3-5b30-b5e4-f2553a9d2491'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: In August 1966, Apollo soars from Cape Kennedy.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/25/1966-apollo-soars-from-cape/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: In August 1966, Apollo soars from Cape Kennedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAPE KENNEDY — The United States today [Aug. 25] rocketed an unmanned Apollo moon ship three-quarters of the way around the world to a Pacific Ocean splash down. The 44,700-pound Apollo rode atop a powerful Saturn-1 rocket that landed 93 minutes after leaving here. The Apollo zoomed to a peak altitude of 700 miles over South Africa. Then its own rocket engine fired to drive it back, heat shield first, into the earth’s atmosphere at 19,000 miles per hour. Apollo control reported the 11,000-pound capsule impacted south-west of the aircraft carrier Hornet, the prime recovery ship, some 300 miles southeast of Wake Island.— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Aug. 26, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/blogs/26oldiht-1966-apollo/26oldiht-1966-apollo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/26/blogs/26oldiht-1966-apollo/26oldiht-1966-apollo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 766}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/blogs/26oldiht-1966-apollo/26oldiht-1966-apollo-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'leg