rticle/e00dfc26-b3f8-55a8-b578-f59dc32d3215', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e00dfc26-b3f8-55a8-b578-f59dc32d3215'}, {'abstract': 'The Italian wall lizard, a reptile native to Europe that has been in the New York area for decades and is moving north, is a case study in foreign species’ adaptability to urban life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/26/nyregion/migrant-lizard-having-made-it-in-new-york-heads-elsewhere.html', 'snippet': 'The Italian wall lizard, a reptile native to Europe that has been in the New York area for decades and is moving north, is a case study in foreign species’ adaptability to urban life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For centuries, New York City has been the gateway to a new land, and now residents believed to have originated around warm, sunny Tuscany are moving on after a few years of city life. How they managed to get by in this colder, more crowded climate is being studied by Colin Donihue, a biologist doing postdoctoral studies at Harvard University.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/nyregion/26ABOUT1/26ABOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/26/nyregion/26ABOUT1/26ABOUT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/nyregion/26ABOUT1/26ABOUT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/nyregion/26ABOUT1/26ABOUT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption':