e': 'Ziegesar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07e91b53-6087-53dc-994b-b277ae568120', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07e91b53-6087-53dc-994b-b277ae568120'}, {'abstract': 'Nora Raleigh Baskin’s “Nine, Ten” and Jewell Parker Rhodes’s “Towers Falling” bring the events surrounding the 2001 terror attacks to a new group of readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/books/review/nine-ten-nora-raleigh-baskin-towers-falling-jewell-parker-rhodes.html', 'snippet': 'Nora Raleigh Baskin’s “Nine, Ten” and Jewell Parker Rhodes’s “Towers Falling” bring the events surrounding the 2001 terror attacks to a new group of readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NINE, TEN: A SEPTEMBER 11 STORYBy Nora Raleigh Baskin197 pp. Atheneum. $16.99. (Middle grade; ages 8 to 12)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two New Novels Succeed in Telling the Story of 9/11 to Kids', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Day in September', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baskin, Nora Raleigh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Nine, Ten: A September 11 Story (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rhodes, Jewell Parker (1954- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Towers Falling (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-26T14:56:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eliot Schrefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eliot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schrefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9c8316e-a106-5741-b00e-027ab8588c33', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9c8316e-a106-5741-b00e-027ab8588c33'}, {'abstract': 'Even when finely translated, not all good books travel well across cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/books/review/tim-parks-laments-the-excessive-influence-of-international-book-fairs.html', 'snippet': 'Even when finely translated, not all good books travel well across cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s one world, we’re one community, and so we must all read the same books. Otherwise how could we talk about them to one another? Thus the logic of globalization: ever larger numbers of people worldwide reading the very best (or best-promoted?) authors. It’s good news for those authors and their publishers, not so encouraging for the increasing number of people who would like to make a living from writing. And of course it raises the intriguing question: Even when well translated, can we really understand what other cultures are talking about? Will a housewife in Tibet (assuming there are “housewives” in Tibet) make sense of Jonathan Franzen’s St.\u202fPaul, Minn.? Can other people truly get the hang of Bulgarian etiquette or courtship in Laos or street life in the Philippines? Are we interested? Does it make sense to be interested? In short, do all good books travel?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/0828-BKS-PARKS/0828-BKS-PARKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/0828-BKS-PARKS/0828-BKS-PARKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarg