c1f27a1-f439-5883-b47d-be7db826dcca'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times Stylebook is our canonical reference for how our newsroom uses language. During our recent Maker Week, we asked: how might we make the online Stylebook even more useful?', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/26/putting-style-into-the-online-new-york-times-stylebook/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times Stylebook is our canonical reference for how our newsroom uses language. During our recent Maker Week, we asked: how might we make the online Stylebook even more useful?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1895, the editors of The New York Times created the inaugural version of the paper’s Manual of Style and Usage — a guidebook to the publication’s particular rules of grammar, punctuation, spelling and capitalization that remains an essential part of our newsroom toolkit. Since then, it’s been updated regularly to reflect the changing times (the word email, for example, appeared as early as 1985 and was styled as “e-mail”). In 1999, the first online version of the manual, known as the Stylebook, became available on the NYT intranet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting {Style} into the Online New York Times Stylebook', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-26T16:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrei Kallaur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kallaur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e28298-7af6-5cff-9469-dd46292ee4dd', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://articl