_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/27/arts/music/rudy-van-gelder-essential-recordings.html', 'snippet': 'Tracing Mr. Van Gelder’s imprint on recordings like Coltrane’s “Moment’s Notice” or the Miles Davis Quintet’s “If I Were a Bell,” among others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a musical hero of towering influence dies, the urge is to go straight to the tape: recordings, footage, a captured moment that stands in for the unwieldy fullness of a life.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/27/arts/26RUDY/26RUDY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Rudy Van Gelder Shaped the Sound of Jazz as We Know It', 'kicker': 'An Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He Quietly, Steadily Shaped the Sound of Jazz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Van Gelder, Rudy (1924-2016)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-26T16:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Appraisal', '_id': 'nyt://article/326f3b84-0744-51fa-960f-f35aeccfa303', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/326f3b84-0744-51fa-960f-f35aeccfa303'}, {'abstract': 'Colson Whitehead, whose “The Underground Railroad” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, cites “The Twilight Zone” as a key influence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Colson Whitehead, whose “The Underground Railroad” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, cites “The Twilight Zone” as a key influence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifth Dimension: Remember John Cheever’s story “The Swimmer”? Sure you do: That’s the one where a guest at a garden party decides on a lark to make his way home by water, swimming one by one across his neighbors’ pools only to find that a year or more seems to pass in the space of a single afternoon. As a surreal gesture — the journey not just through geography but through time — that premise has something in common with Colson Whitehead’s novel “The Underground Railroad” (No.\u202f1 in its third week on the hardcover fiction list), in which a runaway slave encounters various examples of racism on her flight north that could come only from a more contemporary America. Yet Whitehead was influenced less by Cheever, he told me via email, than by “another ’60s artifact of pop fabulism”: “I can work back through my life,” he said, “and pick out key moments where I was exposed to some new fabulist gesture — ‘One Hundred Years of Solitude’ in 12th grade, the ‘Battle Royal’ chapter of ‘Invisible Man’ in a \xadseventh-grade English class — but my earliest immersion into the fantastic was probably reruns of ‘The Twilight Zone.’ Slave girl Cora emerging from the literal underground railroad and seeing a skyscraper: That’s a quintessential ‘Twilight Zone’ moment, where you take a step in the wrong direction and suddenly you’re in a world a little off to the side.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/books/review/28whitehead-list/28whitehead-list-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitehead, Colson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-26T16:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0acd7ff3-8cf7-5371-88e9-93da3169bfe0', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0acd7ff3-8cf7-5371-88e9-93da3169bfe0'}, {'abstract': 'The death of the August vacation for psychotherapists underlines the cultural decline of the therapeutic profession in the life of New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/28/nyregion/a-rite-of-august-shrinks-away-for-some-new-yorkers.html', 'snippet': 'The death of the August vacation for psychotherapists underlines the cultural decline of the therapeutic profession in the life of New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On July 31, 1967, The New York Times ran an article, on Page 29, headlined “Vacations Begun by Psychiatrists.” It appeared that day among articles with headlines like “Pravda Article Assails 