'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Egypt Goes to the Beach', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Egypt Goes to the Beach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beaches', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathing Suits and Swimwear', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Veiling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-28T22:42:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mona Eltahawy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eltahawy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/62f21963-7241-515b-a55a-7c54259b576a', 'word_count': 1039, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62f21963-7241-515b-a55a-7c54259b576a'}, {'abstract': 'Spaniards can’t agree on who should rule the country, but they are united in one thing: disgust for politicians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/29/opinion/spain-a-country-with-no-government.html', 'snippet': 'Spaniards can’t agree on who should rule the country, but they are united in one thing: disgust for politicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — On Jan. 2, 1492, Isabella of Castile and Ferdinand of Aragon — known as the Catholic Monarchs — occupied Granada, completing their conquest of Moorish Spain. Ever since, Spain has always had a government — and occasionally two, when Napoleon invaded in 1808, and during the 1936 to 1939 civil war that split it. But never during those more than five centuries was it ever without any. That is, until Dec. 20 last year, when elections failed to give any party the majority needed to form a government and all attempts at a coalition failed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 468, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 468}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/26/opinion/26caparros/26caparros-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1246, 'width': 1598, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spain: A Country With No Government', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A country with no government', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ciudadanos Party (Spain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Popular Party (Spain)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Podemos (Spanish Political Party)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Spanish Socialist Workers' Party", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-28T22:50:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martín Caparrós', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martín', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caparrós', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/861427b0-49ee-5879-8dd9-c463995b4ec3', 'word_count': 1075, 'uri': 'nyt://article/861427b0-49ee-5879-8dd9-c463995b4ec3'}, {'abstract': 'Colombians should support their government’s peace accord with the FARC and end an era of suffering that took some 220,000 lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/29/opinion/the-best-deal-for-colombia.html', 'snippet': 'Colombians should support their government’s peace accord with the FARC and end an era of suffering that took some 220,000 lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1983, guerrilla fighters from the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia murdered Alberto Uribe in a botched kidnapping attempt. Two decades later, Alberto Uribe’s son, Álvaro, would serve as Colombia’s president, from 2002-10, leading the government in a civil war against the rebel group he held responsible for his father’s death.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/28/opinio