': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-29T20:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/faa3904e-1405-5e77-8ad0-2ab539028934', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faa3904e-1405-5e77-8ad0-2ab539028934'}, {'abstract': 'With a new striking suite of pieces inspired by 3D-modeling software glitches, Christopher Stuart is one to watch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/29/t-magazine/design/christopher-stuart-furniture-designer.html', 'snippet': 'With a new striking suite of pieces inspired by 3D-modeling software glitches, Christopher Stuart is one to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, Christopher Stuart, an ex-welder based in Indianapolis, captured the attention of the design world with a deceptively simple piece of furniture called the U Bench, made from four conjoined, smoky black sheets of steel. Earlier this year, he got his first show, “Constructs & Glitches,” at the Future Perfect, featuring a striking suite of furniture inspired by snags he encountered while using 3D modeling software. Rather than troubleshoot the bugs — which added surface anomalies and amusing protuberances to his renderings — Stuart incorporated them, challenging the bedrock design principle that form follows function. “Function doesn’t always need to be utilitarian,” he says. “Isn’t the way something makes you feel also a kind of function?”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '70', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/t-magazine/design/stuart2/stuart2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/29/t-magazine/design/stuart2/stuart2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight'