AN, France — In December, the French photographer Marie Dorigny arrived in Lesbos, Greece, to document the refugee crisis there. Many of the migrants, fleeing war-torn countries and landing by boat, were women and children whose needs posed special challenges to the European Union, the United Nations and various nongovernmental organizations struggling to cope. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a French Photographer Made Intimate Photos of Female Refugees', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorigny, Marie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-30T08:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26679ba6-4cfd-59cd-ae79-94e373ccac9a', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26679ba6-4cfd-59cd-ae79-94e373ccac9a'}, {'abstract': 'What do you think of Beyoncé and the messages of her visual album “Lemonade”?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/08/30/picture-prompt-beyonc-at-the-v-m-a-s/', 'snippet': 'What do you think of Beyoncé and the messages of her visual album “Lemonade”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are you a Beyoncé fan? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/08/29/us/29xp-mothers/29xp-mothers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Picture Prompt | Beyoncé at the V.M.A.s', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-08-30T08:19:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa749c69-2653-5582-82cb-874f11b2f710', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa749c69-2653-5582-82cb-874f11b2f710'}, {'abstract': 'What Donald Trump is really borrowing from Richard Nixon’s campaign rhetoric.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/magazine/america-is-safer-than-it-used-to-be-so-why-do-we-still-have-calls-for-law-and-order.html', 'snippet': 'What Donald Trump is really borrowing from Richard Nixon’s campaign rhetoric.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By its own historical standards, America circa 2016 is a safe place. The country’s violent crime rate is about half of what it was in 1991. Cities, in particular, have become markedly less dangerous. Less than half as many police officers are killed in the line of duty today as in the mid-1970s. In 1968, Americans rated “crime and lawlessness” as the single most important domestic problem facing the nation. Today, according to Gallup, they rank “crime/violence” below issues like the economy, unemployment, racism and race relations, and dissatisfaction with government.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 586, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 586}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04firstwords/04firstwords-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2001, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'America Is Safer Than It Used to Be. So Why Do We Still Have Calls for ‘Law and Order’?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Riot Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gage, Beverly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Richard Milhous', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coolidge, Calvin', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-30T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beverly Gage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beverly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01e009b3-7057-5c9a-ba00-31b0abf354c7', 'word_count': 1520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01e009b3-7057-5c9a-ba00-31b0abf354c7'}, {'abstract': 'Most people think granola is a health food. But commercial varieties of it are often loaded with enough added sugar to rival a slice of chocolate cake.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/30/well/eat/why-your-granola-is-really-a-dessert.html', 'snippet': 'Most people think granola is a health food. But commercial varieties of it are often loaded with enough added sugar to rival a slice of chocolate cake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1863, Dr. James Caleb Jackson, a staunch nutrition advocate, created the world’s first recipe for what would become granola. The dish (he called it granula) consisted of dense, unsweetened bran nuggets, soaked in milk.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/science/wellgranola/wellgranola-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1254, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Your Granola Is Really a Dessert', 'kicker': 'Eat Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cereals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Foods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kellogg, John Harvey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sugar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, James Caleb (1811-95)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-30T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Eat', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/892a8158-8ef3-5c4f-9407-fa99c47b0dec', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/892a8158-8ef3-5c4f-9407-fa99c47b0dec'}, {'abstract': 'Pictures that capture a moment in history sometimes contain a glint of magic, an element of wonder that can never be fully understood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/magazine/the-mystery-at-the-heart-of-great-photographs.html', 'snippet': 'Pictures that capture a moment in history sometimes contain a glint of magic, an element of wonder that can never be fully understood.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There is nothing as mysterious as a fact clearly described.” The fact that versions of this observation have been attributed to two very different street photographers, Garry Winogrand and Lisette Model, underlines its wisdom and its mystery. It helps explain why attempts to stage photographs — to create fictions — only rarely work as powerfully as the kind of quotations from reality that we get in documentary photographs. Larry Sultan once said he “always thought of a great photograph as if some creature walked into my room; it’s like, how did you get here? ... The more you try to control the world, the less magic you get.” Winogrand had no objection to staging things; it was just that he could never come up with anything as interesting as what was out there in the streets. But when does the staging start?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04onphoto1/04mag-04onphoto-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04onphoto1/04mag-04onphoto-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04onphoto1/04mag-04onphoto-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04onphoto1/04mag-04onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04onphoto1/04mag-04onphoto-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'ju