 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Appraisal', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3f25c62-0cad-5720-a36d-1a9f8bd1d41c', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3f25c62-0cad-5720-a36d-1a9f8bd1d41c'}, {'abstract': 'A string of killings of black men by the police has been the impetus for filmmakers and television producers to re-examine this 1955 murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/08/31/movies/in-era-of-black-lives-matter-films-focus-on-emmett-till-lynching.html', 'snippet': 'A string of killings of black men by the police has been the impetus for filmmakers and television producers to re-examine this 1955 murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simeon Wright, nearly 13, a Mississippi sharecropper’s son, shared a bed with his 14-year-old cousin, Emmett Till, on the fateful night of Aug. 28, 1955. Till, visiting from Chicago, was taken from the room that night by two white intruders and, in the next few terrible hours, became the victim of one of the most infamous racial crimes in American history, when he was tortured and murdered for supposedly whistling at a white woman.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/07/13/arts/31TILL/31TILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 760, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/07/13/arts/31TILL/31TILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 760}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/07/13/arts/31TILL/31TILL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 809, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/07/13/arts/31TILL/31TILL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1994, 'width': 1574, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': 