ct', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'What in the World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Feng', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feng', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16ade6b7-a2f8-5884-8c7e-170a2aa19917', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16ade6b7-a2f8-5884-8c7e-170a2aa19917'}, {'abstract': 'Why Berhanu Nega traded a tenured position for the chance to lead a revolutionary force against an oppressive regime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/magazine/once-a-bucknell-professor-now-the-commander-of-an-ethiopian-rebel-army.html', 'snippet': 'Why Berhanu Nega traded a tenured position for the chance to lead a revolutionary force against an oppressive regime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Berhanu Nega was once one of Bucknell University’s most popular professors. An Ethiopian exile with a Ph.D. from the New School for Social Research in Manhattan, he taught one of the economics department’s most sought-after electives, African Economic Development. When he wasn’t leading seminars or puttering around his comfortable home in a wooded neighborhood five minutes from the Bucknell campus in rural Lewisburg, Pa., Nega traveled abroad for academic conferences and lectured on human rights at the European Parliament in Brussels. “He was very much concerned with the relationship between democracy and development,” says John Rickard, an English professor who became one of his close friends. “He argued that you cannot have viable economic development without democratization, and vice versa.” A gregarious and active figure on campus, he rooted for the Philadelphia Eagles and the Cleveland Cavaliers, campaigned door-to-door for Barack Obama in 2008 and was known as one of the best squash players on the Bucknell faculty. He and his wife, an Ethiopian-born optometrist, raised two sons and sent them to top-ranked colleges, the University of Pennsylvania and Carnegie Mellon. On weekends he sometimes hosted dinners for other Bucknell professors and their families, regaling them with stories about Abyssinian culture and history over Ethiopian food he would prepare himself; he imported the spices from Addis Ababa and made the injera, a spongy sourdough bread made of teff flour, by hand.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04mag-04professor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04mag-04professor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04mag-04professor-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04professor_portrait-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 947, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04professor_portrait-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 947}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04professor_portrait-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 649, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04professor_portrait/04professor_portrait-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1297, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Once a Bucknell Professor, Now the Commander of an Ethiopian Rebel Army', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Professor Nega’s War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammer, Joshua', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Eritrea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ethiopia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nega, Berhanu (1958- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bucknell University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ginbot 7 (Ethiopia)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8550caf5-144f-579f-bd42-683b3e89e981', 'word_count': 4963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8550caf5-144f-579f-bd42-683b3e89e981'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether the deceptions of your employer implicate you, when to tell your boss that a co-worker is a serial sex harasser and what to do about the swindler in the family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/magazine/should-a-teacher-at-a-sketchy-college-help-recruit-students.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether the deceptions of your employer implicate you, when to tell your boss that a co-worker is a serial sex harasser and what to do about the swindler in the family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a professor at a university that has experienced serious financial problems in the last five years. As a result, our university has brought in a number of “consultants,” many of whom work largely for for-profit universities. In response to their advice, the university has significantly shifted its profile, its mission and its ways of doing business. In short, we now accept everyone who applies to the school, and professors and administrators have been strong-armed into passing almost everyone, regardless of how shoddy their work is. I am embarrassed that people are being issued degrees and sent into the work force without the prerequisite skills to do their jobs. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04ethicist/04mag-04ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 382, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/magazine/04ethicist/04mag-04ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 382, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should a Teacher at a Sketchy College Help Recruit Students?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Teach at a Very Sketchy College. Should I Help Recruit Students?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d30b53d6-15fb-5268-b78c-4f73a8ad22aa', 'word_count': 1468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d30b53d6-15fb-5268-b78c-4f73a8ad22aa'}, {'abstract': 'Major League Baseball, the N.F.L. and the N.B.A. announce their schedules only months before their seasons start. Arizona and Nebraska have scheduled a game for 2031.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/01/sports/ncaafootball/busy-schedule-some-college-football-games-give-fans-more-than-a-decade-to-plan.html', 'snippet': 'Major League Baseball, the N.F.L. and the N.B.A. announce their schedules only months before their seasons start. Arizona and Nebraska have scheduled a game for 2031.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Major League Baseball announces every team’s schedule each year about seven months before the season begins. The N.F.L. does it roughly five months in advance, and the N.B.A. waits until just a few months before the opening tip.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALLPIX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALLPIX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALLPIX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/sports/01-FOOTBALLPIX/01-FOOTBALL-superJumbo.j