ight': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/business/01DB-LAW/01DB-LAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/01/business/01DB-LAW/01DB-LAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/business/01DB-LAW/01DB-LAW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/business/01DB-LAW/01DB-LAW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Female Lawyer’s Gender-Bias Suit Challenges Law Firm Pay Practices', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gender-Bias Suits Target Law Firms’ Pay Practices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chadbourne & Parke', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olson, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Kerrie L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T18:06:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Olson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Olson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d293c9e6-ecfb-5e7b-aefe-ff47efe4dde6', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d293c9e6-ecfb-5e7b-aefe-ff47efe4dde6'}, {'abstract': 'An artists known for his maximalist ambition, Mr. Aitken has a show opening in Los Angeles called “Electric Earth.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/arts/design/its-colossal-its-stupendous-its-doug-aitken.html', 'snippet': 'An artists known for his maximalist ambition, Mr. Aitken has a show opening in Los Angeles called “Electric Earth.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2013, Doug Aitken, the internationally acclaimed creator of video and sound installations, produced “Station to Station,” a collaboration with multitudes of other well-known artists, musicians and performers. Called “a nomadic cultural incubator, cross-continental happening and moving earthwork,” it took place on a customized train that traveled from Brooklyn to San Francisco, pausing along the way in Pittsburgh, Santa Fe and five other stops to entertain locals with artworks and performances. It was just one example of Mr. Aitken’s maximalist ambition to make spectacular art commensurate with, yet critical of, the technologically accelerated complexity of modern life. Opening Saturday, Sept. 10, at the Museum of Contemporary Art, Los Angeles, “Doug Aitken: Electric Earth” will survey his two-decade career. Anchored by seven enveloping installations, the exhibition will include single-screen films, photographs and electrified sculptural constructions. (Through Jan. 15, moca.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 226, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 226}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE4/04GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Colossal! It’s Stupendous! It’s Doug Aitken!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; It’s Colossal!  It’s Stupendous!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Doug Aitken: Electric Earth (Exhibit)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aitken, Doug', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T18:07:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f327331-9094-56e5-8ca3-4626b27072da', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f327331-9094-56e5-8ca3-4626b27072da'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Morris’s Dance Center hosts its annual day of free activities in Fort Greene, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/arts/dance/mark-morris-open-house-pastries-classes-and-yes-dancing.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Morris’s Dance Center hosts its annual day of free activities in Fort Greene, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been 15 fruitful years since the Mark Morris Dance Center planted itself in Fort Greene to bring high-quality dance to Brooklyn. The organization hosts its annual open house with a day of free activities on Saturday, Sept. 10, beginning at 9 a.m. with coffee and pastries. (Dancing does require sustenance.) Events continue throughout the day with classes for children and adults in a wide variety of styles: ballet, modern, Gaga, salsa, tap and Afro-Caribbean. Participants will also have the opportunity to learn sections from Mr. Morris’s holiday classic, “The Hard Nut,” which returns to the Brooklyn Academy of Music in December. But for those who prefer to watch, there is a special attraction: The Mark Morris Dance Group will perform Mr. Morris’s ravishing 1995 dance “Pacific” at 1 and 3 p.m. After each performance, there will be appearances by students from the company’s Urban Rhythms and Arts Immersion programs. And there’s a beer garden, courtesy of Sixpoint Brewery, on the center’s fourth-floor terrace. Cross your fingers for sun. (mmdg.org/openhouse.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE5/04GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark Morris Open House: Pastries, Classes and, Yes, Dancing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; A Chance to Move, With Ample Treats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morris, Mark, Dance Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T18:11:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d64ac03e-47cf-5cac-9f8b-7e9231f613e1', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d64ac03e-47cf-5cac-9f8b-7e9231f613e1'}, {'abstract': 'In the latest series to draw its story from the music world, FX and Donald Glover take a comedic approach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/arts/television/chasing-rap-dreams-in-atlanta-with-a-sense-of-humor.html', 'snippet': 'In the latest series to draw its story from the music world, FX and Donald Glover take a comedic approach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A number of television writers seem to have simultaneously realized that the music world is a rich vein for stories, and thus we’ve had “Empire,” “Vinyl,” “Roadies” and “Sex&Drugs&Rock&Roll.” On Tuesday, Sept. 6, FX adds to the list with “Atlanta,” a series that banks on a droll sense of humor to set it apart. The show is about a would-be Atlanta rapper named Alfred Miles (Brian Tyree Henry) who calls himself Paper Boi. Donald Glover, an oddball rapper in real life known as Childish Gambino, who is also the creator of the series, plays Paper Boi’s cousin Earn, who wants to be his manager. But Paper Boi is skeptical that Earn is tough enough. “I need a Malcolm,” he tells him in the premiere. “You’re too Martin. You know what they did to him? They killed him.” To which Earn replies, “Didn’t they kill Malcolm, too?”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE2/04GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1546, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chasing Rap Dreams in ‘Atlanta,’ With a Sense of Humor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television; Rap Dreams in ‘Atlanta’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FX (TV Network)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glover, Donald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henry, Brian Tyree', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Atlanta (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T18:13:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94bf1e03-dd14-5165-807f-273d15fea029', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94bf1e03-dd14-5165-807f-273d15fea029'}, {'abstract': 'Beyoncé’s Formation World Tour comes to MetLife Stadium this week, and Kanye West plays two nights at Madison Square Garden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/arts/music/beyonce-and-kanye-west-tours-bring-intimacy-to-the-big-stage.html', 'snippet': 'Beyoncé’s Formation World Tour comes to MetLife Stadium this week, and Kanye West plays two nights at Madison Square Garden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two of this year’s most structurally impressive tours arrive in the area to remind viewers that big spaces don’t always have to feel that way. Beyoncé has been playing stadiums on her Formation World Tour, using a handful of tricks to shrink them — a gargantuan rotating screen onstage and a runway extension that cuts through the middle of the field. But the most intimate moments are the ones where she stands totally alone, forcing everyone’s gaze right on her. Kanye West has a similar strategy in mind on his Saint Pablo Tour, with a different approach. He has done away with a traditional stage altogether and instead delivers his performance atop a set of platforms hovering over the crowd. It places him eerily close to almost the whole audience, but it also isolates him, a hero and a loner. (Beyoncé, MetLife Stadium, Wednesday, Sept. 7; Kanye West, Madison Square Garden, Monday and Tuesday.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/04GUIDE3/04GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1475, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyoncé and Kanye West Tours Bring Intimacy to the Big Stage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Adding Intimacy to the Big Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Beyonce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'West, Kanye', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MetLife Stadium (NJ)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-08-31T18:15:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f14f1f9-2e1e-58d6-8896-91964e4d6b6a', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f14f1f9-2e1e-58d6-8896-91964e4d6b6a'}, {'abstract': 'This event, organized by Lucy Dhegrae, features singers like the soprano Julia Bullock and works by David Hertzberg, among others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/arts/music/resonant-bodies-festival-showcases-contemporary-vocal-music.html', 'snippet': 'This event, organized by Lucy Dhegrae, features singers like the soprano Julia Bullock and works by David Hertzberg, among others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shaped anew each year by the tastes of its artists, the Resonant Bodies Festival, an immersion in contemporary vocal music organized by the intrepid Lucy Dhegrae, has swiftly become an annual delight. The three festival concerts — Tuesday, Sept. 6; Wednesday; and Thursday — each feature three singers who have programmed their own sets. Among the highlights are appearances by the rich-voiced mezzo-soprano Abigail Fischer, the tenor Peter Tantsits, the authoritative bass-baritone Dashon Burton and the soprano Julia Bullock (fresh from her new evening of Josephine Baker arrangements at Lincoln Center), with works by Lukas Foss; John Cage; and the young, talented David Hertzberg. The flutist and soprano Alice Teyssier, the composer and vocalist Sofia Jernberg, the daring Frauke Aulbert, Sophie Burgos and Charlotte Mundy round out what is always a fascinatingly varied series. (Roulette, Boerum Hill, Brooklyn; 917-267-0363, roulette.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/04/arts/